WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:38.679 --> 00:00:39.179
France,

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.483
July 23,

00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:44.182
1944,

00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:45.123
8.45 a.m.

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:46.159
Lucienne,

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:46.700
my light,

00:00:47.661 --> 00:00:47.941
Jeannot,

00:00:47.981 --> 00:00:48.442
my love,

00:00:49.763 --> 00:00:53.286
it hurts when I think of those wonderful waking moments in Rue de Rennes,

00:00:53.787 --> 00:00:55.028
early in our romance.

00:00:56.409 --> 00:00:57.931
How happy I was to be your lover,

00:00:57.991 --> 00:00:58.531
to see you.

00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:01.114
However more wonderful it was,

00:01:01.774 --> 00:01:03.516
how splendid and patient you were,

00:01:04.518 --> 00:01:05.698
how we craved affection.

00:01:07.807 --> 00:01:10.349
Think my life of those days on French soil,

00:01:11.228 --> 00:01:14.631
and tell yourself that we shall enjoy a wonderful reunion in France,

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:15.730
and a fresh,

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:16.871
passionate honeymoon.

00:01:18.512 --> 00:01:19.472
I'm in your arms,

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.253
my young and splendid lover,

00:01:22.013 --> 00:01:23.134
mother to my Jeannot,

00:01:23.655 --> 00:01:24.314
my comrade,

00:01:24.974 --> 00:01:26.536
my essential shining light.

00:01:27.676 --> 00:01:31.578
But we shall keep our love fresh and not forget how tough it is to be apart.

00:01:32.777 --> 00:01:33.938
I promise you my light,

00:01:34.378 --> 00:01:35.879
you who need no promises.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.621
that you will be reunited with the youngest,

00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:39.463
most ardent,

00:01:39.662 --> 00:01:41.144
craziest lover and beloved.

00:01:42.887 --> 00:01:44.728
How badly I want to be near you now.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:46.911
Kiss our little kid for me.

00:01:48.311 --> 00:01:48.513
Sim.

00:01:58.207 --> 00:01:59.029
Tour du Monde,

00:01:59.308 --> 00:02:00.310
autour d'une table.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.070
Cécilia Reeves,

00:02:02.072 --> 00:02:02.572
de la BBC,

00:02:03.032 --> 00:02:03.512
William Hague,

00:02:03.552 --> 00:02:04.853
de la radio diffusion en Suisse,

00:02:05.194 --> 00:02:05.853
Tim Coppens,

00:02:05.874 --> 00:02:07.234
de la Voix des Etats-Unis d'Amérique,

00:02:07.556 --> 00:02:08.256
Hubert Neumann,

00:02:08.376 --> 00:02:09.697
de la KRO Hollande,

00:02:10.198 --> 00:02:11.117
Mirek Wierdlicki,

00:02:11.277 --> 00:02:12.299
de la radio polonaise,

00:02:12.759 --> 00:02:15.882
vous invitent à effectuer en leur compagnie le Tour du Monde,

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:17.103
autour d'une table.

00:02:20.225 --> 00:02:21.887
Présentation de Jacques Poliak.

00:02:40.891 --> 00:02:44.974
What is this incredible thing I'm hearing?

00:02:45.014 --> 00:02:45.174
Cécile,

00:02:45.175 --> 00:02:48.796
you have just heard the voice of a future participant in our World Tour show,

00:02:48.836 --> 00:02:51.038
the young Richard Simon,

00:02:51.039 --> 00:02:52.419
5 years old,

00:02:52.479 --> 00:02:53.620
born in Paris,

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.962
son of a French mother and a father from America,

00:02:57.423 --> 00:02:59.043
our friend Sim Coppens Bravo

00:03:08.471 --> 00:03:09.431
I had no choice.

00:03:10.012 --> 00:03:11.592
I was on the radio from birth.

00:03:12.552 --> 00:03:13.593
Not surprising really.

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:14.853
My dad,

00:03:14.973 --> 00:03:15.354
Simon,

00:03:15.675 --> 00:03:17.855
was a jazz disc jockey for 50 years.

00:03:19.537 --> 00:03:23.038
This is a special program of American music for our French listeners.

00:03:27.391 --> 00:03:32.158
Some of you who are listening to us tonight recognized this voice.

00:03:32.237 --> 00:03:33.118
20 years ago,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.883
a few thousand French people heard it between the Normandy and Paris coasts.

00:03:36.884 --> 00:03:38.664
And this voice is that of Simcoe Ponce,

00:03:38.705 --> 00:03:40.347
the most French of the Americans.

00:03:41.048 --> 00:03:44.890
If you're a bit like the French Benjamin Franklin,

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.991
you won't know if you're Parisian or American.

00:03:47.252 --> 00:03:50.854
My role was to walk around the Cote d'Antin with a truck.

00:03:50.855 --> 00:03:55.196
A truck that was a kind of radio station,

00:03:55.376 --> 00:03:56.817
an ambulance.

00:03:56.818 --> 00:03:58.058
I played music,

00:03:58.298 --> 00:03:59.018
a lot of music,

00:03:59.058 --> 00:04:01.160
especially a lot of great orchestras.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:10.159
It was in my parents'

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:16.624
house in the Dordogne Valley that I found 63 letters dating from 1944,

00:04:16.625 --> 00:04:19.504
written by my father and addressed to Lucienne,

00:04:19.605 --> 00:04:20.066
my mother.

00:04:22.086 --> 00:04:25.788
My father first came to Lanzac in July 1945.

00:04:26.088 --> 00:04:27.028
An American soldier,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:28.350
he came here with a friend.

00:04:29.271 --> 00:04:30.271
Much to the villagers'

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:30.891
surprise,

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:33.173
they improvised a baseball game.

00:04:34.072 --> 00:04:34.614
That night,

00:04:34.653 --> 00:04:35.194
in a café,

00:04:35.954 --> 00:04:38.656
Simon sang the praises of Franco-American friendship.

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:45.751
My parents must have had this memory in mind when they bought this house late in life.

00:04:46.912 --> 00:04:49.714
The chances were against Simon and Lucienne meeting,

00:04:50.275 --> 00:04:51.015
falling in love,

00:04:51.576 --> 00:04:54.138
and living out their days on the banks of the Dordogne.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:56.380
She,

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:57.561
a French Catholic,

00:04:57.641 --> 00:04:59.702
had only gone to secondary school.

00:05:00.397 --> 00:05:00.517
He,

00:05:00.557 --> 00:05:01.398
an American Jew,

00:05:01.618 --> 00:05:02.339
had a PhD.

00:05:05.623 --> 00:05:07.385
They couldn't have been further apart,

00:05:07.865 --> 00:05:09.146
3,000 miles,

00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:10.548
the width of an ocean.

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:12.230
For the

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:13.531
30s, that was huge,

00:05:13.992 --> 00:05:16.635
with him in New York and her in Soissons,

00:05:16.795 --> 00:05:17.576
north of Paris.

00:05:24.365 --> 00:05:30.850
Lucienne is 17 and lives much to her dislike with her father and stepmother in Soissons.

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:33.972
She has been living with them since she was seven,

00:05:35.153 --> 00:05:36.754
ever since her parents separated.

00:05:37.514 --> 00:05:38.195
Tough times.

00:05:40.396 --> 00:05:46.301
Her father has exiled her to the attic to be rid of her and her bad moods until she comes of age.

00:05:47.982 --> 00:05:48.662
You're dead to me,

00:05:49.183 --> 00:05:49.643
he says.

00:05:50.624 --> 00:05:51.644
You no longer exist.

00:05:52.721 --> 00:05:54.442
When he says that his daughter is dead,

00:05:55.403 --> 00:05:56.744
he knows what he's talking about.

00:05:58.325 --> 00:05:59.746
Lucienne's father is a mortician.

00:06:02.167 --> 00:06:03.128
Lucienne hates him,

00:06:03.949 --> 00:06:04.709
with all her might,

00:06:04.769 --> 00:06:05.710
with all her heart,

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.693
just as she hates the whole stifling bourgeois world around her.

00:06:11.094 --> 00:06:14.456
Her father has always refused to enroll her in high school.

00:06:15.997 --> 00:06:17.938
A girl just has to be able to sew.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:21.040
She isn't of his world.

00:06:22.001 --> 00:06:25.063
She's gone into a position in her hideout under the roof.

00:06:33.327 --> 00:06:34.348
She won't go to Mass.

00:06:35.188 --> 00:06:36.529
She'll never go to Mass again.

00:06:37.049 --> 00:06:38.550
A dead girl doesn't go to Mass.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:40.851
She's got very long braids.

00:06:41.912 --> 00:06:43.793
Two braids that won't go to Mass.

00:06:45.454 --> 00:06:46.194
In three months,

00:06:46.674 --> 00:06:48.435
in exactly 96 days,

00:06:48.895 --> 00:06:51.237
she'll cut them off and leave this place.

00:06:51.873 --> 00:06:52.734
She'll be 18,

00:06:53.254 --> 00:06:55.995
and she won't have to hide her books under her mattress anymore.

00:07:26.489 --> 00:07:28.210
I told you that my mother worked here.

00:07:28.211 --> 00:07:28.270
Yes.

00:07:28.271 --> 00:07:30.951
But before working here,

00:07:30.952 --> 00:07:32.312
you need your glasses.

00:07:32.392 --> 00:07:35.093
You carefully left them in the office.

00:07:35.113 --> 00:07:37.654
It's just a little bit of a mess.

00:07:37.694 --> 00:07:48.278
This letter is from 30 July 1932,

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:53.601
sent under the leadership of Nougaret de Lestra in Soissons.

00:07:54.829 --> 00:07:57.710
I signed and certified that Miss

00:07:58.511 --> 00:07:59.211
Lucienne

00:08:00.071 --> 00:08:11.897
Godiard was employed in our house from May 1st 1928 to this day and that she gives us all commitment with a beautiful signature.

00:08:13.385 --> 00:08:21.871
And I have a duty to add to this certificate that we are ready to give the best,

00:08:21.872 --> 00:08:23.432
I don't know if it's recommendations,

00:08:23.433 --> 00:08:24.053
I have trouble,

00:08:24.353 --> 00:08:25.814
on Mademoiselle Gaudiard,

00:08:26.174 --> 00:08:28.756
excellent employee in every way,

00:08:28.836 --> 00:08:32.779
very appreciated by the clients who regret it like us.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:33.940
I read.

00:08:36.001 --> 00:08:37.281
Barely arrived in Paris,

00:08:37.882 --> 00:08:39.682
Lucienne finds a job in a bookstore.

00:08:40.382 --> 00:08:41.863
Not any old bookstore either.

00:08:42.803 --> 00:08:45.404
The Gallimard Bookstore in Boulevard Raspail,

00:08:46.084 --> 00:08:46.544
no less.

00:08:49.645 --> 00:08:50.465
She loves books,

00:08:51.306 --> 00:08:52.286
even closed ones.

00:08:53.106 --> 00:08:53.886
She sniffs them,

00:08:54.287 --> 00:08:55.147
she strokes them.

00:08:56.107 --> 00:08:56.707
It's physical.

00:08:57.487 --> 00:08:58.988
The smell of ink and paper.

00:08:59.728 --> 00:09:01.269
The perfection of the guillotine.

00:09:02.509 --> 00:09:04.570
The sound of pages peeling apart.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:07.891
The weight of every story and every life.

00:09:09.491 --> 00:09:11.692
And the sight of the city through the window.

00:09:12.393 --> 00:09:13.773
People enter and leave.

00:09:14.593 --> 00:09:15.234
It's alive.

00:09:16.834 --> 00:09:19.255
Vous avez le roman d'un tricheur de Sacha Guitry?

00:09:19.635 --> 00:09:19.755
Oui,

00:09:20.095 --> 00:09:20.495
vous l'avez?

00:09:21.516 --> 00:09:23.717
Le vin de solitude d'Irene Némirovski?

00:09:25.057 --> 00:09:27.219
And this comedian who follows her everywhere,

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:27.979
he smells the soup,

00:09:28.019 --> 00:09:30.001
she doesn't like him.

00:09:30.021 --> 00:09:30.881
He's stupid,

00:09:30.882 --> 00:09:31.622
he reads Le Figaro.

00:09:33.283 --> 00:09:35.225
André Gide passed yesterday,

00:09:35.226 --> 00:09:36.225
it's her who served him.

00:09:36.866 --> 00:09:37.366
He looked worried.

00:09:37.367 --> 00:09:40.547
He spoke quickly and did not pay.

00:09:41.527 --> 00:09:42.028
On leaving,

00:09:42.029 --> 00:09:42.288
he said,

00:09:42.428 --> 00:09:43.408
Thank you,

00:09:43.508 --> 00:09:43.888
miss,

00:09:44.048 --> 00:09:44.688
for your help.

00:09:44.689 --> 00:09:46.409
He gave her a brief,

00:09:46.969 --> 00:09:47.769
very brief look,

00:09:47.849 --> 00:09:52.111
and stretched his hand towards her face as if to tap her cheek.

00:09:52.991 --> 00:09:53.911
Instinctively,

00:09:53.951 --> 00:09:54.731
she moved back.

00:09:54.771 --> 00:09:58.593
He did not look surprised.

00:09:58.613 --> 00:09:59.853
It shocked her that he did not pay.

00:09:59.893 --> 00:10:01.894
She would like to tell someone.

00:10:17.212 --> 00:10:19.313
Claudia spelling books.

00:10:24.176 --> 00:10:26.617
Myrtle lived the young Tarantine.

00:10:27.698 --> 00:10:30.379
Her ship carried her to the banks of the Camerine.

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:32.441
There would marriage,

00:10:32.621 --> 00:10:33.661
songs and flutes.

00:10:35.182 --> 00:10:37.344
have slowly led her to her lover's gates.

00:10:37.364 --> 00:10:43.608
The vigilante key had locked away the wedding garland and set her for this day.

00:10:56.978 --> 00:10:59.280
Words go round and round in Lucien's mouth.

00:11:00.101 --> 00:11:01.362
They are as soft as pebbles.

00:11:02.362 --> 00:11:05.283
They'll be nice and ready when the man of her life comes knocking.

00:11:06.464 --> 00:11:07.825
Then he'll lie on top of her,

00:11:08.385 --> 00:11:10.146
as on a beach in the late afternoon.

00:11:11.706 --> 00:11:14.988
He'll feel the warmth she's been storing up all these years.

00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:19.970
In the meantime,

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:20.651
she smokes.

00:11:21.111 --> 00:11:22.111
Balto cigarettes.

00:11:22.492 --> 00:11:23.412
American flavor.

00:11:24.593 --> 00:11:26.774
She used to hate the smell of tobacco in Soissons.

00:11:27.746 --> 00:11:28.446
Here in Paris,

00:11:28.526 --> 00:11:29.167
it's different.

00:11:29.427 --> 00:11:30.167
It excites her.

00:11:30.867 --> 00:11:31.768
It envelops her.

00:11:32.268 --> 00:11:33.528
It puts her mind at rest.

00:11:34.909 --> 00:11:37.030
She stubs out her cigarette in a tin can.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:38.991
She hears the sounds of the city.

00:11:40.351 --> 00:11:42.252
The big city she's always dreamed of.

00:12:11.714 --> 00:12:13.998
Simon was studying law at Brown University.

00:12:14.759 --> 00:12:15.480
He was bored.

00:12:16.742 --> 00:12:18.966
One of his professors helped him get a scholarship,

00:12:19.547 --> 00:12:19.867
saying,

00:12:20.228 --> 00:12:22.091
Go to France and learn French.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:40.792
He sets off to a foreign land with foreigners.

00:12:41.893 --> 00:12:43.535
He speaks Yiddish and English,

00:12:44.395 --> 00:12:45.957
no more than three words of French.

00:12:47.878 --> 00:12:53.984
Simon may tell himself that his father and mother crossed the Atlantic 30 years earlier in the other direction,

00:12:54.644 --> 00:12:55.985
from Lithuania to America,

00:12:56.966 --> 00:12:59.689
but he still feels very alone in his gray suit.

00:13:01.851 --> 00:13:03.472
Everyone around him is smiling,

00:13:03.912 --> 00:13:04.913
so he smiles too.

00:13:05.814 --> 00:13:08.416
as though it were nothing to leave behind his family,

00:13:09.036 --> 00:13:09.476
his city,

00:13:10.277 --> 00:13:10.937
his friends.

00:13:11.757 --> 00:13:13.258
Nothing to switch languages.

00:13:52.229 --> 00:13:54.190
From his rented room in Rue Soufflot,

00:13:54.911 --> 00:13:57.993
Simon photographs the state funeral of Paul Dumers,

00:13:58.634 --> 00:14:00.375
the assassinated French president,

00:14:00.996 --> 00:14:02.497
passing under his window.

00:14:05.318 --> 00:14:10.180
He was revising quietly when the hubbub of the crowd distracted him from his conjugations.

00:14:12.061 --> 00:14:12.922
It isn't much fun,

00:14:13.322 --> 00:14:13.982
this language,

00:14:14.042 --> 00:14:16.283
with as many exceptions as there are rules.

00:14:17.304 --> 00:14:18.464
The exam is on Monday.

00:14:19.204 --> 00:14:22.306
He's got to finish first or second to keep his scholarship.

00:14:23.386 --> 00:14:25.607
He's not remotely competitive by nature.

00:14:26.608 --> 00:14:27.828
He wants everyone to win,

00:14:28.609 --> 00:14:29.769
everyone to come first.

00:14:32.446 --> 00:14:33.450
You could say he's shy,

00:14:34.233 --> 00:14:35.840
but that's not quite the right word.

00:14:37.542 --> 00:14:38.803
Let's say he's reserved.

00:14:39.584 --> 00:14:41.545
Like a table in a restaurant is reserved.

00:14:43.407 --> 00:14:44.888
Someone's going to come along.

00:14:45.628 --> 00:14:47.910
Someone's going to sit down next to him.

00:14:49.211 --> 00:14:50.352
Someone's going to help him.

00:14:51.213 --> 00:14:52.554
Reveal him to himself.

00:14:59.279 --> 00:15:01.141
When he returns to the United States,

00:15:01.541 --> 00:15:02.141
he's in love.

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:03.931
Not with a girl,

00:15:04.832 --> 00:15:06.534
but with friends.

00:15:50.042 --> 00:15:53.805
Simon's hoarse from shouting outside the campus library all afternoon.

00:15:54.886 --> 00:15:59.390
He and two fellow students have organized a rally in support of the Spanish Republic.

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:03.033
He's been handing out flyers and collected $12.

00:16:04.033 --> 00:16:04.534
Not bad.

00:16:06.215 --> 00:16:07.997
Several students stopped to talk to him.

00:16:08.757 --> 00:16:09.638
Only one muttered,

00:16:10.499 --> 00:16:12.120
I sure hope Franco wipes you out,

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:13.201
you dirty commie Jew.

00:16:14.646 --> 00:16:16.507
He doesn't know what annoys him more,

00:16:17.108 --> 00:16:20.070
Franco's brutality or his neighbor's indifference.

00:16:21.751 --> 00:16:23.613
He knows he's jeopardizing his PhD.

00:16:25.134 --> 00:16:25.914
Horatio Smith,

00:16:26.155 --> 00:16:27.616
the head of the French department,

00:16:28.216 --> 00:16:30.157
who helps him get a scholarship every year,

00:16:30.978 --> 00:16:33.500
doesn't really appreciate this charivari.

00:16:35.281 --> 00:16:36.362
That's the word he used,

00:16:37.043 --> 00:16:37.663
charivari.

00:16:39.064 --> 00:16:40.645
Simon looked up the definition.

00:16:41.746 --> 00:16:42.687
A lot of noise.

00:16:43.368 --> 00:16:44.088
That suits him.

00:16:44.869 --> 00:16:47.511
He's making a lot of noise for the Spanish Republicans.

00:16:48.693 --> 00:16:49.213
Not enough,

00:16:49.293 --> 00:16:49.834
some would say.

00:16:52.997 --> 00:16:53.918
It started raining.

00:16:54.858 --> 00:16:55.939
The campus empties.

00:16:57.521 --> 00:16:58.402
Tuckville awaits.

00:17:17.842 --> 00:17:18.683
Okay,

00:17:18.783 --> 00:17:20.686
requesting FBI records.

00:17:20.706 --> 00:17:21.086
There we go.

00:17:22.949 --> 00:17:23.229
Okay,

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:23.950
so...

00:17:26.012 --> 00:17:26.453
Dear...

00:17:27.354 --> 00:17:29.034
Freedom of Information Officer.

00:17:30.895 --> 00:17:31.015
So,

00:17:31.695 --> 00:17:33.216
date range request.

00:17:33.556 --> 00:17:37.637
I always thought my father was under surveillance by the FBI.

00:17:38.437 --> 00:17:39.637
As I'm no longer a U.S.

00:17:39.697 --> 00:17:40.137
citizen,

00:17:40.237 --> 00:17:44.339
I asked my cousin Ruthie to help me to officially apply to see his file.

00:17:45.099 --> 00:17:48.920
I imagined memos about him being tailed and secret meetings,

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:49.920
a romantic,

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:50.960
shadowy past.

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:51.881
Let me see.

00:17:53.941 --> 00:17:54.701
involvement,

00:17:54.801 --> 00:17:55.622
affiliations,

00:17:55.702 --> 00:17:56.482
memberships.

00:17:57.202 --> 00:18:00.304
Possible surveillance during McCarthy period,

00:18:00.305 --> 00:18:01.384
1940.

00:18:01.385 --> 00:18:02.065
I think that's,

00:18:03.105 --> 00:18:03.265
well,

00:18:03.266 --> 00:18:04.906
that's basically what I'm looking for.

00:18:05.587 --> 00:18:05.867
Okay.

00:18:06.387 --> 00:18:07.688
Should we just mail it in?

00:18:08.108 --> 00:18:08.468
Mail it.

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:18.614
But the only document I find is a text written for an international conference on the eve of World War II.

00:18:19.474 --> 00:18:19.774
In it,

00:18:19.894 --> 00:18:20.454
my father,

00:18:20.854 --> 00:18:22.615
an American Student Union delegate,

00:18:23.015 --> 00:18:26.115
analyzes Jefferson's links to the French Revolution.

00:18:27.136 --> 00:18:28.496
No hint of communism,

00:18:29.336 --> 00:18:31.456
just an article by a good student.

00:18:33.777 --> 00:18:35.397
Going through the papers yesterday,

00:18:35.817 --> 00:18:39.198
I think you saw a song that I wrote at that time called

00:18:39.638 --> 00:18:40.958
Love in the YCL.

00:18:41.739 --> 00:18:42.379
Would you sing it?

00:18:42.779 --> 00:18:43.019
Sure.

00:18:43.679 --> 00:18:48.480
And that's why members who use to talk about finances.

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:52.745
Now go around discussing their romances.

00:18:53.145 --> 00:18:56.570
There's a new intrigue within the league.

00:18:56.990 --> 00:18:58.031
It's love in the

00:18:58.512 --> 00:18:59.613
YCL.

00:19:00.715 --> 00:19:03.478
Fellas and girls are starting to...

00:19:03.678 --> 00:19:08.562
discover there's no better place to find your ideal lover.

00:19:08.982 --> 00:19:12.745
It's a treat to be kissed by a young communist.

00:19:13.326 --> 00:19:14.307
It's love in the

00:19:14.727 --> 00:19:15.708
YCL.

00:19:16.608 --> 00:19:20.512
Oh, there's a new kid at all our meetings now,

00:19:21.072 --> 00:19:24.074
equipped with bow and arrow.

00:19:24.895 --> 00:19:28.478
It's little Cupid sending greetings now,

00:19:28.878 --> 00:19:33.322
and all our Marxist-Leninists are now becoming feminists.

00:19:34.343 --> 00:19:35.924
No time to waste,

00:19:36.365 --> 00:19:38.206
just sitting around debating.

00:19:38.707 --> 00:19:42.631
Get into the league and start your Christmas mating.

00:19:43.051 --> 00:19:46.615
There's a partner cute for each new recruit.

00:19:47.195 --> 00:19:48.196
It's love in the

00:19:48.696 --> 00:19:49.677
YCL.

00:19:51.759 --> 00:19:57.365
This is a picture of active members of the American Student Union.

00:19:58.065 --> 00:20:01.266
And one of the activities that we were involved in,

00:20:01.346 --> 00:20:08.588
which was raising money to send ambulances to the loyalist side in Spain.

00:20:09.048 --> 00:20:15.150
And the campaign on behalf of Spain was a very popular one at that time.

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:19.171
We had many methods of collecting the money.

00:20:20.011 --> 00:20:22.992
One of them was to go on the subways.

00:20:23.172 --> 00:20:24.772
My brother Mo and

00:20:25.132 --> 00:20:27.253
I have memories of the two of us.

00:20:28.333 --> 00:20:39.198
going on subways and speaking to the people on the subways and telling them the story of Spain and collecting money,

00:20:39.318 --> 00:20:44.761
which we used to send to the loyalist causes.

00:20:45.101 --> 00:20:48.682
And we played a role in that campaign.

00:20:57.901 --> 00:21:04.826
The Hebrew Rite Rumba rumba rumba The Hebrew Rite Rumba rumba rumba

00:21:05.286 --> 00:21:07.427
One night I spent Ay,

00:21:07.588 --> 00:21:08.348
call her,

00:21:09.089 --> 00:21:12.671
call her One night I spent

00:21:13.252 --> 00:21:27.589
I can't I can't I can't I can't I can't I can't I can't I can't

00:21:57.613 --> 00:22:02.075
Aikamela,

00:22:02.095 --> 00:22:04.597
Aikamela To give culture to the people and the people to culture.

00:22:05.517 --> 00:22:08.378
This is the goal of the association to which Lucienne has just joined.

00:22:09.079 --> 00:22:13.141
An association made on purpose for paid leave and to knit the alliance,

00:22:13.221 --> 00:22:14.722
as it is written in the statutes,

00:22:15.062 --> 00:22:18.283
between intellectuals and popular classes.

00:22:18.284 --> 00:22:19.344
The popular classes,

00:22:19.384 --> 00:22:20.124
Lucienne repeats,

00:22:20.125 --> 00:22:20.745
are a whole program.

00:22:21.589 --> 00:22:24.931
The bed of the bourgeoisie and the popular layers.

00:22:24.932 --> 00:22:25.351
This year,

00:22:25.471 --> 00:22:27.352
the corsages are around the neck,

00:22:27.353 --> 00:22:28.173
freeing the shoulders.

00:22:28.853 --> 00:22:30.454
And Lucienne has beautiful,

00:22:30.494 --> 00:22:33.176
very beautiful shoulders.

00:22:33.177 --> 00:22:35.577
We urge to ride in the air to say that we believe in it,

00:22:35.578 --> 00:22:37.818
that youth brings happiness and that the old world will change.

00:22:38.699 --> 00:22:41.561
Lucienne feels at home among these people.

00:22:41.562 --> 00:22:43.622
She has finally found a family,

00:22:43.982 --> 00:22:44.382
her family,

00:22:44.383 --> 00:22:46.023
a sensual and concerned family.

00:22:55.987 --> 00:22:56.787
That's them there,

00:22:57.208 --> 00:22:58.389
on top of the haystack.

00:22:59.450 --> 00:23:00.190
Part of a group,

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:00.851
at last.

00:23:02.112 --> 00:23:04.234
She got to the top first and began to sing.

00:23:05.034 --> 00:23:07.076
Then everyone climbed up and sang along,

00:23:07.596 --> 00:23:10.178
and she thought that love must strike this powerfully,

00:23:10.439 --> 00:23:10.859
this high.

00:23:11.579 --> 00:23:19.805
that this was what she longed for exactly this this emotion inspired by all the buddies clambering onto the haystacks

00:23:20.285 --> 00:23:37.196
20 feet up and singing believing in a better world based on values other than power exploitation and money the man next to her was singing to his name is Pascal and she loves his best voice

00:23:38.837 --> 00:23:40.858
She held onto his shoulders for the photo.

00:23:41.598 --> 00:23:43.179
She felt a ping in her stomach.

00:23:44.659 --> 00:23:46.700
Her foot slipped and he just about caught her.

00:23:47.361 --> 00:23:49.462
Another inch and she'd have tumbled to the bottom.

00:23:50.822 --> 00:23:51.602
It made her laugh.

00:23:58.305 --> 00:23:58.946
She shouts,

00:23:59.706 --> 00:24:00.666
Freedom for Spain

00:24:01.747 --> 00:24:02.287
He yells,

00:24:02.807 --> 00:24:04.148
Freedom for the whole world

00:24:05.628 --> 00:24:06.189
She thinks.

00:24:07.245 --> 00:24:09.647
Freedom for lips to love and bodies to mingle.

00:25:04.157 --> 00:25:07.161
Look at the body.

00:25:08.343 --> 00:25:10.626
An extremely elongated body.

00:25:10.646 --> 00:25:11.247
Very fusing.

00:25:12.489 --> 00:25:17.492
You can understand why someone like Giacometti was shaped.

00:25:17.493 --> 00:25:19.093
So we have this ascending effect,

00:25:19.113 --> 00:25:21.455
which is very linear,

00:25:21.515 --> 00:25:22.936
very romantic.

00:25:22.996 --> 00:25:23.576
And yet,

00:25:23.577 --> 00:25:25.998
things that are already in the Gothic spirit,

00:25:26.018 --> 00:25:28.800
look at the fact that we rediscover the volumes of the human body.

00:25:28.801 --> 00:25:30.581
I think of the third woman,

00:25:30.641 --> 00:25:30.981
who is,

00:25:30.982 --> 00:25:31.982
as we leave the door,

00:25:32.743 --> 00:25:33.903
probably the Queen of Saba.

00:25:34.404 --> 00:25:34.664
You see,

00:25:34.665 --> 00:25:35.384
you have a small belly,

00:25:35.385 --> 00:25:38.286
a small overgrowth of the chest.

00:25:38.306 --> 00:25:39.567
The neighbors,

00:25:39.727 --> 00:25:41.008
the two men who are neighbors,

00:25:41.068 --> 00:25:41.849
probably...

00:25:42.609 --> 00:25:43.410
David and Solomon,

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:47.552
you see under the dress something on the knee.

00:25:47.553 --> 00:25:50.734
We are actually rediscovering the human body and that's a very big moment.

00:25:51.594 --> 00:25:52.555
It's true of faces.

00:25:52.735 --> 00:25:54.376
So the faces are at the same time very erratic,

00:25:54.377 --> 00:25:55.876
as if they had come from the night of time,

00:25:55.976 --> 00:25:56.357
fixed.

00:25:56.358 --> 00:25:59.018
And at the same time you have the beginning of a smile.

00:25:59.138 --> 00:25:59.278
Well,

00:25:59.279 --> 00:26:00.459
this same woman smiles.

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:01.640
It may seem nothing,

00:26:01.641 --> 00:26:04.461
but it is one of the first smiles that reappears in the history of sculpture for several centuries.

00:26:04.481 --> 00:26:05.702
So it's a real blow of tenacity.

00:26:17.956 --> 00:26:25.466
The

00:26:31.001 --> 00:26:35.423
What's very surprising is that I have four photos of the day the first time my parents met,

00:26:35.763 --> 00:26:37.223
which is quite rare.

00:26:37.224 --> 00:26:37.343
Yes.

00:26:37.403 --> 00:26:38.864
Because of this conference,

00:26:38.984 --> 00:26:43.425
this visit organized by a popular front organization called Peuple et Culture.

00:26:43.445 --> 00:26:49.427
The two came without knowing each other,

00:26:49.487 --> 00:26:53.149
they met there and they did not leave each other until their death.

00:26:54.521 --> 00:26:57.503
What surprised me was that there are pictures of this visit,

00:26:57.504 --> 00:26:58.344
which become postcards.

00:26:58.345 --> 00:27:00.626
It must have been used at the time,

00:27:00.666 --> 00:27:01.206
I don't know.

00:27:02.407 --> 00:27:06.850
And so my mother wrote the name of the speaker and the date,

00:27:07.151 --> 00:27:09.352
which obviously is important,

00:27:09.353 --> 00:27:09.953
and the place,

00:27:10.113 --> 00:27:10.693
obviously,

00:27:11.294 --> 00:27:12.014
important too.

00:27:21.233 --> 00:27:21.553
It's him,

00:27:22.393 --> 00:27:23.574
just behind Lucienne.

00:27:24.234 --> 00:27:24.554
Simon,

00:27:24.634 --> 00:27:25.114
it's Simon.

00:27:26.535 --> 00:27:28.095
His belly to him and his back to her,

00:27:28.096 --> 00:27:30.836
gathered by a thin layer of air.

00:27:33.257 --> 00:27:33.957
Him,

00:27:33.977 --> 00:27:38.658
perfectly focused on what's going to happen.

00:27:38.659 --> 00:27:38.778
Her,

00:27:38.818 --> 00:27:41.559
eager to know and to feel.

00:27:41.579 --> 00:27:43.680
They don't know yet.

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:47.041
They haven't raised their voice.

00:27:47.061 --> 00:27:47.961
They should shake it a little.

00:27:48.825 --> 00:27:50.465
She walks on his foot by my guard.

00:27:50.526 --> 00:27:52.626
She should introduce herself.

00:27:54.567 --> 00:27:56.747
Lucienne turns around.

00:27:56.947 --> 00:28:00.609
She doesn't smile like any other comrade would.

00:28:00.610 --> 00:28:04.170
The intensity of her look is intimidating.

00:28:04.171 --> 00:28:06.530
Simon looks serious.

00:28:06.570 --> 00:28:07.030
Almost absent.

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:11.812
Both of them focus on the speaker's words.

00:28:11.832 --> 00:28:13.032
We would like to say,

00:28:13.033 --> 00:28:14.453
there is before.

00:28:15.773 --> 00:28:17.174
There is after,

00:28:17.175 --> 00:28:18.154
like a page turning,

00:28:18.155 --> 00:28:18.914
a flower blooming.

00:28:18.934 --> 00:28:19.934
But no,

00:28:19.935 --> 00:28:21.635
it will happen more slowly,

00:28:21.675 --> 00:28:21.795
well,

00:28:22.175 --> 00:28:23.556
a little more slowly.

00:28:39.169 --> 00:28:44.033
He who enters the labyrinth moves seven times back and forth,

00:28:44.473 --> 00:28:45.053
in and out,

00:28:45.994 --> 00:28:47.916
seven times back and forth,

00:28:48.736 --> 00:28:52.079
running his tongue around her mouth seven times before speaking.

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:56.342
Seven times his tongue reaches right to the back of her mouth,

00:28:57.323 --> 00:28:58.003
the last step,

00:28:58.563 --> 00:29:00.465
otherwise known as the leap of joy.

00:29:01.906 --> 00:29:03.047
His tongue on her palate,

00:29:04.108 --> 00:29:05.288
such a delicious mouth.

00:29:07.305 --> 00:29:08.806
Simon has removed his beret.

00:29:09.567 --> 00:29:10.527
He has beautiful,

00:29:10.847 --> 00:29:11.968
oyster-colored eyes.

00:29:12.909 --> 00:29:16.711
Lucienne greets him like someone tossing a puck to start a game of hopscotch.

00:29:17.692 --> 00:29:21.235
The way one rubs the box against the ground so it slides better,

00:29:21.975 --> 00:29:23.856
aiming for the square that stands for heaven.

00:29:25.457 --> 00:29:27.759
Lucienne makes sure she doesn't tread on the lines.

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:30.461
Simon says a couple of words.

00:29:31.181 --> 00:29:32.242
His accent is nice.

00:29:33.563 --> 00:29:34.203
He's nice.

00:29:34.824 --> 00:29:35.504
Really nice.

00:29:36.969 --> 00:29:40.472
Simon goes into his shell the further he moves through the labyrinth.

00:29:42.194 --> 00:29:43.215
Are you in Paris for long?

00:29:44.096 --> 00:29:45.277
Are you staying for a while?

00:29:47.118 --> 00:29:47.699
She loves me.

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:50.321
Lucienne's voice echoes inside him.

00:29:51.222 --> 00:29:51.983
She loves me not.

00:29:53.064 --> 00:29:53.604
She loves me.

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:30.775
Simon applied for a scholarship to complete his PhD in Paris and his professors approved it.

00:30:32.096 --> 00:30:37.681
Where better than the National Library to study French views on American democracy under the Second Empire?

00:30:39.022 --> 00:30:39.682
Day after day,

00:30:39.762 --> 00:30:40.363
he reads the book.

00:30:40.443 --> 00:30:44.306
newspapers from the period and reread Tocqueville over and over again.

00:30:45.747 --> 00:30:47.689
But Simon actually has something else in mind.

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:50.151
As soon as he arrives in Paris,

00:30:50.892 --> 00:30:52.693
he goes to Trade Union House,

00:30:53.074 --> 00:30:57.337
the dispatching office for volunteers who want to fight in the Lincoln Brigade.

00:30:59.199 --> 00:31:00.260
The comrades put him off.

00:31:01.320 --> 00:31:03.582
Franco's forces may be at the gate of Madrid,

00:31:04.123 --> 00:31:05.824
but no more brigadiers are wanted.

00:31:10.319 --> 00:31:13.861
When he mentioned the refugees to the pretty brunette he met in Chartres,

00:31:14.661 --> 00:31:16.663
she stared at him silently for a while,

00:31:17.603 --> 00:31:22.866
then rummaged in her bag and pulled out a card bearing the colours of the Spanish Republic.

00:31:27.969 --> 00:31:31.090
Lucien and Simon have come up with a way to meet again soon.

00:31:32.311 --> 00:31:35.013
They've undertaken to sponsor two refugee children,

00:31:35.773 --> 00:31:36.734
Miguel and Alma,

00:31:37.234 --> 00:31:38.114
who are eight and ten.

00:31:39.643 --> 00:31:40.643
They're sitting quietly,

00:31:41.243 --> 00:31:43.464
waiting for them at the Casa de España.

00:31:48.526 --> 00:31:49.906
Two Spanish Basque children,

00:31:50.726 --> 00:31:51.507
a French woman,

00:31:52.127 --> 00:31:52.867
and an American.

00:31:53.967 --> 00:31:55.148
A proper little family.

00:32:07.987 --> 00:32:11.113
This colony had been created for orphans,

00:32:11.153 --> 00:32:12.175
for abandoned children,

00:32:12.296 --> 00:32:13.558
for the lost.

00:32:13.598 --> 00:32:15.381
They were almost all Basques.

00:32:18.123 --> 00:32:18.663
The Spanish

00:32:19.123 --> 00:32:19.624
House, Mrs.

00:32:19.764 --> 00:32:20.584
Lola Sacristán,

00:32:20.604 --> 00:32:23.145
worked as an administrator.

00:32:23.146 --> 00:32:24.686
We called her Mrs.

00:32:24.966 --> 00:32:25.446
Lola.

00:32:25.546 --> 00:32:27.467
Among other things,

00:32:27.667 --> 00:32:32.248
she organized the distribution of clothes when Josephine Baker came.

00:32:32.249 --> 00:32:36.730
The famous singer came with a truck full of clothes to give us as a gift,

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:37.230
and Mrs.

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:38.491
Lola distributed them.

00:32:39.451 --> 00:32:43.257
In the colony of the House of Spain we had godparents who were French marriages.

00:32:43.258 --> 00:32:46.762
My brother and I were always godparents together.

00:32:46.802 --> 00:32:50.126
My brother and I had several godparents.

00:32:50.667 --> 00:32:51.508
They gave us gifts,

00:32:51.548 --> 00:32:52.950
took us for walks,

00:32:53.010 --> 00:32:54.633
to the movies,

00:32:54.673 --> 00:32:55.794
to eat...

00:32:56.255 --> 00:32:57.035
I remember a lady,

00:32:57.075 --> 00:32:59.417
who apparently lived alone,

00:33:00.137 --> 00:33:01.438
maybe a widow,

00:33:01.458 --> 00:33:04.059
who took us out a couple of times.

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:07.341
I also vaguely remember a walk through a fair with another godfather.

00:33:07.381 --> 00:33:09.382
The one I remember the most,

00:33:09.483 --> 00:33:11.444
because I spent more time with him,

00:33:11.445 --> 00:33:12.604
was Le Patron.

00:33:12.644 --> 00:33:17.227
They were a couple of low-class social workers,

00:33:18.268 --> 00:33:19.008
communists,

00:33:19.308 --> 00:33:20.389
sensationalists.

00:33:20.409 --> 00:33:22.390
They were very good people,

00:33:22.470 --> 00:33:22.730
very...

00:33:22.790 --> 00:33:23.831
from the town.

00:34:01.449 --> 00:34:01.760
Hello.

00:34:28.843 --> 00:34:31.185
You look at yourself,

00:34:31.186 --> 00:34:38.452
you look at yourself,

00:34:38.492 --> 00:34:52.606
and you look at me again.

00:34:53.707 --> 00:34:54.907
I found this old,

00:34:55.087 --> 00:34:55.748
all-purpose,

00:34:55.948 --> 00:34:57.708
extra-smooth paper notebook.

00:34:58.649 --> 00:34:59.849
The page I am sending you,

00:35:00.189 --> 00:35:01.189
dated August 3,

00:35:01.190 --> 00:35:03.090
1939,

00:35:03.091 --> 00:35:04.290
brings back many memories,

00:35:04.691 --> 00:35:05.391
good memories.

00:35:09.492 --> 00:35:10.052
Last night,

00:35:10.152 --> 00:35:11.033
around 8 o'clock,

00:35:11.533 --> 00:35:13.953
Simon met Francis and me on Boulevard Raspail,

00:35:14.834 --> 00:35:17.735
and we went up to Lucienne Godiard's on the 6th floor.

00:35:19.099 --> 00:35:20.540
Lucienne's room is very sweet,

00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:24.361
decorated in good taste with an excellent selection of books.

00:35:25.901 --> 00:35:26.422
Lucienne,

00:35:26.762 --> 00:35:27.882
whom we like very much,

00:35:28.662 --> 00:35:30.503
had prepared a most delicious dinner.

00:35:31.463 --> 00:35:31.724
Soup,

00:35:32.384 --> 00:35:32.964
roast chicken,

00:35:33.204 --> 00:35:33.904
cauliflower,

00:35:34.184 --> 00:35:34.605
salad,

00:35:35.085 --> 00:35:37.125
a good 1927 Bordeaux,

00:35:37.426 --> 00:35:38.186
chocolate mousse,

00:35:38.226 --> 00:35:38.986
and calvados,

00:35:39.266 --> 00:35:39.987
very strong.

00:35:41.387 --> 00:35:41.908
After dinner,

00:35:41.948 --> 00:35:47.959
we chatted for a long time as a lovely full moon rose slowly over the roofs of Paris.

00:35:49.261 --> 00:35:49.722
At last,

00:35:49.742 --> 00:35:50.804
we said our goodbyes,

00:35:50.844 --> 00:35:54.129
and Francis and I took the U-Bus to the Glacier stop.

00:36:14.908 --> 00:36:15.688
It's early summer.

00:36:17.109 --> 00:36:18.410
War is on everyone's lips.

00:36:19.491 --> 00:36:20.412
But it's early summer.

00:36:23.394 --> 00:36:25.315
Simon can think of nothing but Lucienne.

00:36:26.797 --> 00:36:28.178
When he reaches the small room,

00:36:28.238 --> 00:36:29.459
he's totally paralyzed.

00:36:30.720 --> 00:36:34.302
He doesn't move while she undoes his shirt button by button.

00:36:35.383 --> 00:36:38.105
He doesn't move while she strokes his chest.

00:36:39.166 --> 00:36:40.127
as if he were a girl,

00:36:40.187 --> 00:36:40.687
he thinks,

00:36:41.027 --> 00:36:42.928
as he runs his hand through her loose hair.

00:36:44.529 --> 00:36:45.830
Lucienne's a bit disappointed,

00:36:46.290 --> 00:36:47.531
but of course she doesn't show it.

00:36:48.732 --> 00:36:49.612
A few days later,

00:36:50.152 --> 00:36:51.653
she'll kneel down in front of him,

00:36:51.893 --> 00:36:52.314
and again,

00:36:52.934 --> 00:36:54.055
he'll wonder what she's doing.

00:36:55.275 --> 00:36:58.957
He backs away and makes various excuses to go off and catch his breath.

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:03.051
Lucien says nothing for now.

00:37:03.851 --> 00:37:04.672
She doesn't push it.

00:37:05.653 --> 00:37:06.793
Slips under the covers.

00:37:07.394 --> 00:37:08.575
And turns on her side.

00:37:09.675 --> 00:37:10.696
He kisses her back.

00:37:11.416 --> 00:37:12.437
He kisses her hands.

00:37:13.578 --> 00:37:14.579
He's like a little boy.

00:37:15.819 --> 00:37:17.040
He knows nothing of love.

00:37:45.925 --> 00:37:52.871
Thank

00:37:52.872 --> 00:37:59.797
you.

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:08.891
Honeymoon,

00:38:09.651 --> 00:38:10.432
under the bombs.

00:38:11.012 --> 00:38:12.654
That might be a nice song title,

00:38:13.154 --> 00:38:14.976
but living through it is a different thing.

00:38:16.257 --> 00:38:17.458
They're barely reunited,

00:38:17.938 --> 00:38:19.139
yet already they must part.

00:38:20.800 --> 00:38:25.124
Simon has to return to America because the United States is closing its borders.

00:38:26.925 --> 00:38:29.487
Simon translates the circular he's just received.

00:38:30.068 --> 00:38:31.089
From September 10th,

00:38:31.829 --> 00:38:36.433
only citizens with a passport and married couples may enter the U.S.

00:38:39.274 --> 00:38:40.315
I'll never get married.

00:38:40.795 --> 00:38:41.216
You hear me,

00:38:41.296 --> 00:38:41.676
Simon?

00:38:42.597 --> 00:38:42.917
Never.

00:38:43.337 --> 00:38:44.018
I'd rather die.

00:38:45.699 --> 00:38:47.000
Simon agrees with Lucienne.

00:38:47.861 --> 00:38:49.162
He's read Marx and Engels,

00:38:49.742 --> 00:38:53.665
and has held forth on the family as a pillar of the class-based society.

00:38:54.646 --> 00:38:55.967
Yet he tries to persuade her,

00:38:56.488 --> 00:38:57.728
because everyone around him,

00:38:58.389 --> 00:38:59.070
his parents,

00:38:59.150 --> 00:39:00.551
his brothers and sisters,

00:39:01.051 --> 00:39:01.692
is married,

00:39:02.252 --> 00:39:03.533
and it hasn't killed any of them.

00:39:05.294 --> 00:39:07.315
There are tears and kisses.

00:39:08.136 --> 00:39:09.877
Lucienne won't budge a ninch.

00:39:10.877 --> 00:39:11.237
For her,

00:39:11.557 --> 00:39:13.338
marriage is a synonym for hypocrisy.

00:39:14.359 --> 00:39:16.540
If two people truly love each other,

00:39:16.940 --> 00:39:29.067
then they don't need to get married.

00:39:42.926 --> 00:39:43.326
Monsieur

00:39:44.087 --> 00:39:44.788
Pierre Matarasson,

00:39:46.149 --> 00:39:48.852
accept to take as your wife

00:39:49.473 --> 00:39:50.794
Francine Vormez.

00:39:51.675 --> 00:39:51.795
Yes.

00:39:53.697 --> 00:39:54.778
Francine Vormez,

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:58.242
accept to take as your wife

00:39:58.722 --> 00:39:59.683
Pierre Matarasson.

00:39:59.844 --> 00:39:59.964
Yes.

00:39:59.965 --> 00:40:01.706
In the name of the law,

00:40:01.726 --> 00:40:04.949
I declare you united by the marriage.

00:40:09.918 --> 00:40:11.920
Mayor of the 6th arrondissement of Paris,

00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:16.563
September 1939.

00:40:16.703 --> 00:40:18.084
There is no way to make a dish,

00:40:18.085 --> 00:40:18.484
thinks Simon,

00:40:18.524 --> 00:40:22.547
who is more and more at ease with the French language.

00:40:22.548 --> 00:40:23.468
It is only a formality,

00:40:23.648 --> 00:40:26.150
says Lucienne,

00:40:26.151 --> 00:40:27.951
a formality to cross the border.

00:40:28.392 --> 00:40:30.573
So why does she feel so moved?

00:40:31.354 --> 00:40:32.875
Elle est émue parce qu'elle est aimée.

00:40:34.116 --> 00:40:35.578
Elle est émue quand Simon dit oui,

00:40:36.498 --> 00:40:37.960
même si c'est un oui de papier.

00:40:39.241 --> 00:40:41.142
Ils se connaissent depuis quatre mois seulement.

00:40:41.983 --> 00:40:42.824
La salle est vide,

00:40:43.424 --> 00:40:44.865
immense caisse de résonance.

00:40:47.287 --> 00:40:49.429
Lucienne porte son petit tailleur de travail,

00:40:50.150 --> 00:40:51.571
celui que Miss Gaines aime bien,

00:40:52.272 --> 00:40:53.192
parce qu'il est strict,

00:40:53.313 --> 00:40:53.813
dit-elle,

00:40:53.953 --> 00:40:54.694
et bien taillé.

00:40:55.835 --> 00:40:56.996
Un tailleur bien taillé,

00:40:57.676 --> 00:40:58.777
un foulard de couleur.

00:41:00.158 --> 00:41:01.299
Simon thinks of his father.

00:41:02.440 --> 00:41:03.782
Lucienne is still dazzling.

00:41:04.042 --> 00:41:04.963
Not for the families,

00:41:04.964 --> 00:41:05.644
they are not there.

00:41:06.404 --> 00:41:07.285
Not for the friends,

00:41:07.286 --> 00:41:08.246
they are not invited.

00:41:08.247 --> 00:41:10.909
She is still dazzling for Simon.

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:14.353
It is simple for him to desire it,

00:41:14.453 --> 00:41:16.214
that he cannot resist it.

00:41:17.996 --> 00:41:19.258
And on this plane,

00:41:19.259 --> 00:41:20.739
on this plane very precisely,

00:41:20.779 --> 00:41:22.501
there are progress.

00:42:00.387 --> 00:42:00.952
you

00:43:15.118 --> 00:43:17.380
They did their level best to travel together,

00:43:18.181 --> 00:43:19.262
but it didn't work out.

00:43:20.182 --> 00:43:21.884
Not enough money for Lucien's ticket,

00:43:22.324 --> 00:43:23.465
so Simon goes ahead.

00:43:25.327 --> 00:43:26.568
Simon's eyes are moist.

00:43:27.168 --> 00:43:28.469
It might be the evening drizzle,

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:30.391
or he might be missing Lucien.

00:43:31.412 --> 00:43:32.513
Everything blurs together.

00:43:34.094 --> 00:43:35.095
Burning memories,

00:43:35.515 --> 00:43:36.416
searing memories.

00:43:37.617 --> 00:43:38.598
The disappointments,

00:43:39.138 --> 00:43:40.139
the deep tenderness,

00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:42.601
the moments of awkwardness.

00:44:10.120 --> 00:44:11.902
When I boarded the boat in Le Havre,

00:44:12.402 --> 00:44:13.383
as my father had,

00:44:13.683 --> 00:44:16.425
to repeat his October 1939 voyage,

00:44:17.346 --> 00:44:19.708
I took with me Stendhal's complete works,

00:44:20.188 --> 00:44:21.869
which he had bought to take to New York.

00:44:24.746 --> 00:44:25.647
Editions du Divan,

00:44:26.107 --> 00:44:30.030
annotated by Henri Martineau in 75 volumes.

00:44:31.070 --> 00:44:32.151
A family legend.

00:44:33.032 --> 00:44:35.233
These books had crossed the Atlantic twice.

00:44:36.054 --> 00:44:40.577
Proof of my father's love of French literature and of his erudition.

00:44:42.418 --> 00:44:44.039
Only none of the books had been read.

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:45.860
The pages were uncut.

00:44:46.681 --> 00:44:47.602
In 60 years,

00:44:48.122 --> 00:44:51.424
neither my father nor anyone else in the family had opened them.

00:44:54.150 --> 00:44:55.653
The crossing took six days,

00:44:55.733 --> 00:44:56.715
so I had time.

00:44:57.396 --> 00:44:59.540
I took a knife and I began.

00:45:40.038 --> 00:45:41.019
For five months,

00:45:41.359 --> 00:45:44.121
Lucienne has saved up and fought for a ship's berth.

00:45:45.862 --> 00:45:48.083
She had to go to Genoa to board the Washington,

00:45:48.584 --> 00:45:50.145
the last direct boat to New York.

00:45:51.686 --> 00:45:53.307
She knew Paris when she left Soissons.

00:45:54.367 --> 00:45:55.808
She had a good job at Gallimard.

00:45:57.209 --> 00:45:58.530
Eight years is quite a lot.

00:46:01.712 --> 00:46:03.193
She can't really imagine America.

00:46:10.362 --> 00:46:13.003
She's leaving everything behind for an unknown world.

00:46:13.944 --> 00:46:14.404
For love.

00:46:17.465 --> 00:46:18.506
She's leaving everything.

00:46:19.747 --> 00:46:21.107
But she finds her father again.

00:46:22.488 --> 00:46:24.829
Sometimes you just have to say you're your father's daughter.

00:46:26.590 --> 00:46:28.311
She has to admit that right now,

00:46:29.011 --> 00:46:33.673
it's good to be the daughter of a French-born Frenchman and a French-born Frenchwoman.

00:46:39.435 --> 00:46:43.537
Lucienne gives her identity for the third time with supporting evidence.

00:46:44.898 --> 00:46:47.018
A series of uniformed men interview her.

00:46:48.059 --> 00:46:49.860
They suspect her of being a Nazi spy.

00:46:51.320 --> 00:46:51.861
A spy?

00:46:54.001 --> 00:46:56.703
And what if they send her back without even letting her off the boat?

00:46:57.463 --> 00:46:58.784
Apparently it's happened before.

00:47:00.644 --> 00:47:01.605
One man keeps at her.

00:47:02.085 --> 00:47:03.306
He speaks a little French.

00:47:04.598 --> 00:47:09.440
Isn't there something fishy about marrying an American citizen on the very cusp of war in Europe?

00:47:21.685 --> 00:47:23.406
Simon's waiting on the quayside for her.

00:47:24.187 --> 00:47:27.988
She saw him waving his hat and blowing kisses to her a little earlier.

00:47:29.969 --> 00:47:30.909
He must be worried.

00:47:32.486 --> 00:47:33.687
He isn't allowed aboard,

00:47:34.248 --> 00:47:35.709
and she isn't allowed to get off.

00:47:36.390 --> 00:47:37.751
It's enough to drive you crazy.

00:47:39.793 --> 00:47:42.115
But she must keep herself from blowing her top.

00:47:43.556 --> 00:47:44.317
Clench her guts,

00:47:44.677 --> 00:47:45.218
keep a grip,

00:47:45.558 --> 00:47:46.159
button down.

00:47:47.880 --> 00:47:50.563
The situation finally looks like it's been resolved.

00:47:51.223 --> 00:47:53.666
Simon is fetched on board to compare their accounts,

00:47:54.546 --> 00:47:56.208
one after the other to begin with,

00:47:56.809 --> 00:47:57.389
then together.

00:47:58.950 --> 00:48:01.312
When he's finally able to take her in his arms,

00:48:01.912 --> 00:48:03.133
she bursts into tears.

00:48:04.534 --> 00:48:05.254
They have a long,

00:48:05.575 --> 00:48:06.095
lingering,

00:48:06.275 --> 00:48:06.976
tender kiss.

00:48:09.397 --> 00:48:11.018
No one can separate them now.

00:48:53.588 --> 00:48:55.549
Now they have to build themselves a nest.

00:48:56.550 --> 00:48:57.351
But before that,

00:48:58.031 --> 00:48:59.012
they have to get married.

00:48:59.712 --> 00:49:00.033
Again.

00:49:00.973 --> 00:49:02.254
With a great pomp this time.

00:49:02.915 --> 00:49:03.755
It's all arranged.

00:49:05.357 --> 00:49:06.297
Lucienne's not angry.

00:49:07.378 --> 00:49:10.079
She's just a bit annoyed that she never wanted to get married,

00:49:10.479 --> 00:49:12.980
but is now on her second wedding in seven months.

00:49:13.840 --> 00:49:14.721
To the same man,

00:49:15.201 --> 00:49:15.881
Simon says,

00:49:16.701 --> 00:49:17.562
that's not too bad,

00:49:17.662 --> 00:49:17.922
is it?

00:49:19.403 --> 00:49:21.323
The first wedding was for the papers.

00:49:21.983 --> 00:49:23.984
The second is to please the family.

00:49:24.784 --> 00:49:26.045
And to please them some more,

00:49:26.625 --> 00:49:28.146
she has to pretend she's Jewish.

00:49:35.970 --> 00:49:36.611
With this ring,

00:49:38.033 --> 00:49:39.574
I join my life with yours.

00:49:40.976 --> 00:49:45.381
My commitment to you is strong and without reservation.

00:49:46.763 --> 00:49:48.925
I take you to be my lifetime partner,

00:49:50.908 --> 00:49:55.213
secure in the knowledge that you will be my constant friend.

00:49:56.930 --> 00:49:58.370
Ani Lado-dee.

00:49:58.390 --> 00:49:58.630
Ani.

00:49:58.631 --> 00:49:59.351
Ani.

00:49:59.671 --> 00:50:01.291
Lado-dee.

00:50:01.292 --> 00:50:01.511
Lado-dee.

00:50:01.571 --> 00:50:01.951
Lado-dee-lee.

00:50:01.952 --> 00:50:02.812
Lado-dee-lee.

00:50:03.052 --> 00:50:03.932
I am my beloved.

00:50:04.252 --> 00:50:05.012
I am my beloved.

00:50:05.013 --> 00:50:06.193
And my beloved is mine.

00:50:06.333 --> 00:50:07.513
And my beloved is mine.

00:50:07.514 --> 00:50:08.693
I taught him everything he knows.

00:50:10.214 --> 00:50:11.354
I take it to be my wife.

00:50:28.022 --> 00:50:30.064
But not only are two people been married,

00:50:30.604 --> 00:50:32.345
but two families have come together.

00:50:32.746 --> 00:50:37.109
And I know you've taken them into your hearts as son and daughter and sister and brother.

00:51:06.015 --> 00:51:06.595
my friends,

00:51:07.256 --> 00:51:10.016
It's also breaking the old life of this couple,

00:51:10.096 --> 00:51:10.997
beginning of a new one.

00:51:12.077 --> 00:51:14.578
There's also an extra prayer involved here.

00:51:15.278 --> 00:51:18.220
That this marriage lasts so the pieces can never come together again.

00:51:18.240 --> 00:51:19.381
You better do a good job.

00:51:21.522 --> 00:51:23.944
It also puts an exclamation point on the ceremony.

00:51:24.004 --> 00:51:25.885
So when you hear the sound of the glass breaking,

00:51:26.245 --> 00:51:27.806
join in with a hearty congratulations.

00:51:28.247 --> 00:51:28.807
Mazel tov,

00:51:28.907 --> 00:51:30.308
or whatever is your expression of joy.

00:51:30.348 --> 00:51:31.969
Watch the rapper's hands when I tell you.

00:51:32.870 --> 00:51:33.710
Three with your heel.

00:51:33.910 --> 00:51:34.090
One,

00:51:34.190 --> 00:51:34.411
two,

00:51:34.551 --> 00:51:34.811
three.

00:51:36.472 --> 00:51:36.672
Uh-oh.

00:51:36.673 --> 00:51:37.273
Mazel tov

00:51:51.399 --> 00:51:56.983
Is she going to have to remember the names of all these people who come up to her to talk in Yiddish?

00:51:57.723 --> 00:52:00.725
For the members of Simon's family talk Yiddish to her,

00:52:01.306 --> 00:52:04.068
and the only thing she can do is to lower her gaze,

00:52:04.588 --> 00:52:05.048
smile,

00:52:05.088 --> 00:52:05.869
and look sweet.

00:52:06.529 --> 00:52:07.609
That's what I'm supposed to do,

00:52:07.769 --> 00:52:08.189
isn't it?

00:52:08.390 --> 00:52:10.690
She asked Simon with a tug on his new tie.

00:52:11.330 --> 00:52:11.930
Looks sweet,

00:52:12.330 --> 00:52:14.051
like having a wife who looks sweet.

00:52:15.071 --> 00:52:16.351
Simon defends himself.

00:52:16.952 --> 00:52:24.793
He warned his family that her awkwardness was due to the fact that French Jews were a little bit different from Lithuanian or American Jews.

00:52:25.554 --> 00:52:26.894
Lucien interrupts him.

00:52:27.514 --> 00:52:28.434
A bit different,

00:52:28.594 --> 00:52:29.395
a bit different,

00:52:29.695 --> 00:52:30.395
big deal.

00:52:31.243 --> 00:52:33.104
Simon leads her over to the stairs.

00:52:33.644 --> 00:52:35.004
She should keep her voice down.

00:52:35.484 --> 00:52:36.685
She has got to trust him.

00:52:37.325 --> 00:52:38.505
Everything will be fine.

00:52:39.005 --> 00:52:42.026
She just has to repeat the right lines on the wedding dais,

00:52:42.547 --> 00:52:44.847
bending her finger after the ring has been put on,

00:52:45.387 --> 00:52:46.628
and drink some wine.

00:52:47.248 --> 00:52:49.289
He'll break the glass by stepping on it.

00:52:50.289 --> 00:52:50.809
Of course,

00:52:50.889 --> 00:52:52.070
Lucien fires back.

00:52:52.570 --> 00:52:53.950
If at least I could break it.

00:53:09.758 --> 00:53:12.000
I wasn't allowed to be a rabbi when I was growing up.

00:53:12.941 --> 00:53:17.045
No women rabbis until the women's movement in 1972.

00:53:17.046 --> 00:53:19.707
The first woman rabbi was was...

00:53:19.867 --> 00:53:22.189
Are you filming this?

00:53:22.650 --> 00:53:23.170
Yeah.

00:53:24.651 --> 00:53:25.632
I have to do a better song.

00:53:25.633 --> 00:53:27.593
And in 1972,

00:53:27.613 --> 00:53:30.175
the first woman rabbi was ordained.

00:53:30.715 --> 00:53:31.456
And at that point,

00:53:31.896 --> 00:53:33.937
I was an escaping housewife from far,

00:53:33.957 --> 00:53:34.357
far away.

00:53:35.478 --> 00:53:35.618
And

00:53:36.199 --> 00:53:37.099
I came to the city,

00:53:37.139 --> 00:53:37.460
and I was,

00:53:37.500 --> 00:53:37.700
like,

00:53:37.840 --> 00:53:38.440
amazed.

00:53:38.961 --> 00:53:39.601
And the women were...

00:53:39.641 --> 00:53:40.102
was happening.

00:53:40.103 --> 00:53:45.048
And I went to my first women's meeting with six pairs of eyelashes and makeup.

00:53:45.088 --> 00:53:46.049
And I said,

00:53:46.050 --> 00:53:46.469
I'm going to,

00:53:46.470 --> 00:53:47.490
I'm an independent woman.

00:53:47.551 --> 00:53:49.353
I came from the suburbs to New York.

00:53:49.473 --> 00:53:50.935
And as soon as they started talking,

00:53:51.455 --> 00:53:52.056
my mouth dry,

00:53:52.296 --> 00:53:54.559
dry open because they were talking about my life.

00:53:55.340 --> 00:53:56.400
life.

00:53:56.401 --> 00:53:57.361
It was so powerful.

00:53:57.421 --> 00:53:58.642
It really informed my life.

00:53:58.643 --> 00:54:00.003
The women's movement did that.

00:54:00.764 --> 00:54:04.167
And then I became aware of what my connection was for all the other struggles,

00:54:04.287 --> 00:54:06.769
all the people that were struggling in the world.

00:54:07.029 --> 00:54:09.111
The black struggle was very important.

00:54:09.731 --> 00:54:11.873
And I feel very connected to that tradition.

00:54:12.313 --> 00:54:13.875
I used to sing with the Black Gospel Choir.

00:54:14.175 --> 00:54:16.036
That was one of the first things I did,

00:54:16.797 --> 00:54:18.438
was sing with the Black Gospel Choir.

00:54:19.039 --> 00:54:24.383
And then the opportunity it came for me to study and I said I want to bring this energy this beautiful and

00:54:24.423 --> 00:54:24.623
energy,

00:54:24.703 --> 00:54:28.867
this connection that these people have to a spirit.

00:54:29.127 --> 00:54:30.768
And I don't care what they call that spirit.

00:54:31.189 --> 00:54:31.909
I want to sing.

00:54:31.969 --> 00:54:34.031
I want to live with that same kind of passion.

00:54:35.072 --> 00:54:38.234
So there's a very strong connection to me between the blues.

00:54:39.195 --> 00:54:41.457
I want to sing the blues.

00:54:41.457 --> 00:54:43.219
blues to gospel,

00:54:43.359 --> 00:54:43.680
you know,

00:54:43.860 --> 00:54:44.040
even,

00:54:44.180 --> 00:54:44.461
I mean,

00:54:44.501 --> 00:54:44.661
it's,

00:54:44.761 --> 00:54:45.001
and the,

00:54:45.181 --> 00:54:46.303
and the cantorial stuff.

00:54:48.305 --> 00:54:53.271
You know,

00:54:53.291 --> 00:54:54.733
there's a very powerful,

00:54:54.853 --> 00:54:56.395
deep emotion that comes from that.

00:54:57.276 --> 00:54:58.377
from both traditions.

00:54:58.877 --> 00:54:59.497
So musically,

00:54:59.498 --> 00:55:01.739
I'm very connected to both those traditions.

00:55:03.140 --> 00:55:04.341
And so the opportunity became,

00:55:04.361 --> 00:55:05.542
because the women's movement,

00:55:05.543 --> 00:55:05.903
they said,

00:55:06.463 --> 00:55:07.464
you can do anything.

00:55:07.844 --> 00:55:09.586
Nobody ever said that to me before.

00:55:10.466 --> 00:55:11.687
So I decided to study.

00:55:11.727 --> 00:55:15.030
The opportunity to become a rabbi came open to me.

00:55:15.610 --> 00:55:18.092
And that's what I did and became a rabbi.

00:55:18.193 --> 00:55:19.814
I don't even know if my father would like it.

00:55:19.815 --> 00:55:20.915
He was so orthodox.

00:55:20.916 --> 00:55:23.917
He was so orthodox?

00:55:24.037 --> 00:55:24.838
So orthodox.

00:55:24.839 --> 00:55:26.239
And he said he wanted to...

00:55:26.279 --> 00:55:27.240
somebody say the Kaddish,

00:55:27.280 --> 00:55:27.900
you know the Kaddish,

00:55:27.901 --> 00:55:28.721
the prayer for the dead.

00:55:29.341 --> 00:55:29.722
And I said,

00:55:29.742 --> 00:55:29.882
well,

00:55:29.902 --> 00:55:30.162
Daddy,

00:55:30.262 --> 00:55:31.003
I can say it.

00:55:31.343 --> 00:55:31.583
He said,

00:55:31.584 --> 00:55:31.703
no,

00:55:31.763 --> 00:55:31.884
no,

00:55:31.944 --> 00:55:32.504
you have to be,

00:55:32.964 --> 00:55:34.225
it needs a man,

00:55:34.265 --> 00:55:35.667
you have to be male to sing it.

00:55:36.127 --> 00:55:37.048
He had no idea.

00:55:38.088 --> 00:55:40.090
And one of the blessings that came to,

00:55:40.091 --> 00:55:40.911
you started me talking,

00:55:40.912 --> 00:55:41.611
can I continue?

00:55:42.752 --> 00:55:44.754
One of the blessings that came to me was that

00:55:46.315 --> 00:55:49.577
I did a wedding for this young woman whose father died,

00:55:49.957 --> 00:55:50.197
you know,

00:55:50.217 --> 00:55:51.218
many years after the wedding,

00:55:51.238 --> 00:55:52.859
and she called me up to do the funeral.

00:55:53.279 --> 00:55:55.880
But her father wanted his rabbi who was from the Chabad.

00:55:55.881 --> 00:55:57.061
It was very orthodox,

00:55:57.921 --> 00:55:59.742
separate places for women to sit.

00:56:01.063 --> 00:56:01.803
And they were arguing.

00:56:01.804 --> 00:56:01.984
I said,

00:56:02.004 --> 00:56:02.604
don't argue.

00:56:02.664 --> 00:56:03.905
Let him do the ceremony,

00:56:04.025 --> 00:56:04.865
and I will talk.

00:56:06.046 --> 00:56:07.446
And when I finished talking,

00:56:07.987 --> 00:56:09.488
when I came off the dais,

00:56:09.868 --> 00:56:10.488
he said to me,

00:56:11.108 --> 00:56:11.469
you know,

00:56:11.529 --> 00:56:11.969
he said,

00:56:12.149 --> 00:56:13.970
we need more rabbis with your neshuma,

00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:14.790
with your soul.

00:56:15.891 --> 00:56:17.932
And I felt that was my father blessing me.

00:56:20.213 --> 00:56:22.655
Because if somebody from the Chabad who was so traditional,

00:56:22.656 --> 00:56:24.295
who was so strict,

00:56:24.876 --> 00:56:25.776
would say that to me,

00:56:25.876 --> 00:56:28.818
then it was a gift to me and for my father.

00:56:29.558 --> 00:56:31.619
And I tell this story because it's so meaningful to me.

00:56:48.508 --> 00:56:49.282
Hello.

00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:32.529
Oh,

00:57:41.247 --> 00:58:03.097
I'm going to be a little bit more serious.

00:59:10.343 --> 00:59:11.264
Oh

00:59:33.419 --> 00:59:34.980
It was simple in the drawing.

00:59:35.300 --> 00:59:35.780
Cervix,

00:59:36.261 --> 00:59:36.641
finger,

00:59:37.221 --> 00:59:37.761
vagina,

00:59:38.302 --> 00:59:41.063
all in black and white and clearly marked with arrows.

00:59:41.664 --> 00:59:46.146
The doctor had told her and Simon had quickly translated what he said.

00:59:46.506 --> 00:59:48.888
The first few times will be a little difficult,

00:59:49.048 --> 00:59:50.989
but then a little difficult.

00:59:51.009 --> 00:59:51.990
You can say that again.

00:59:52.950 --> 00:59:56.012
And then you have to fill the diaphragm with some contraceptive gel,

00:59:56.332 --> 00:59:57.032
but how much?

00:59:57.693 --> 01:00:00.935
And what's the difference between a teaspoon and a coffee spoon?

01:00:01.667 --> 01:00:03.108
Is it bigger or smaller?

01:00:04.349 --> 01:00:05.690
Simon is in the bedroom.

01:00:05.930 --> 01:00:09.073
He can hear Lucienne swearing at the doctor,

01:00:09.233 --> 01:00:11.094
but he doesn't dare go into the bathroom.

01:00:11.835 --> 01:00:12.235
Finally,

01:00:12.315 --> 01:00:15.217
she calls to him and asks him to bring her a teaspoon.

01:00:15.838 --> 01:00:16.979
Can you bring me a teaspoon,

01:00:17.039 --> 01:00:17.439
darling?

01:00:17.979 --> 01:00:19.300
She likes calling him darling.

01:00:19.320 --> 01:00:20.141
It makes her laugh.

01:00:20.621 --> 01:00:22.443
He gently opens the bathroom door.

01:00:25.645 --> 01:00:26.806
Several times that...

01:00:27.987 --> 01:00:33.296
They had my brother and me when they decided to have us.

01:00:33.436 --> 01:00:34.177
So they were planned.

01:00:34.658 --> 01:00:35.379
We were planned.

01:00:36.320 --> 01:00:38.023
A young couple at the beginning of the

01:00:38.885 --> 01:00:39.005
1940s.

01:00:40.487 --> 01:00:41.868
politically involved,

01:00:42.268 --> 01:00:43.709
definitely politically involved,

01:00:44.009 --> 01:00:45.190
how would they deal with that?

01:00:45.610 --> 01:00:48.451
The birth control movement came out of radical roots.

01:00:48.811 --> 01:00:50.312
Margaret Sanger was a radical,

01:00:50.612 --> 01:00:51.233
communist,

01:00:51.413 --> 01:00:52.053
socialist,

01:00:52.233 --> 01:00:52.774
anarchist.

01:00:53.534 --> 01:00:57.476
So anyone traveling in those circles would have known where to go.

01:00:59.357 --> 01:01:02.179
Not all doctors agreed with birth control,

01:01:02.979 --> 01:01:04.740
so you needed to know which one did,

01:01:05.340 --> 01:01:06.781
and you needed to know how to ask.

01:01:06.821 --> 01:01:08.122
But you could certainly get one,

01:01:08.123 --> 01:01:10.303
and you could get contraception legally.

01:01:11.125 --> 01:01:13.109
or you could go to a Planned Parenthood clinic.

01:01:14.375 --> 01:01:20.959
They could have also used withdrawal or abstinence,

01:01:20.979 --> 01:01:25.721
but that's not a desirable way to plan a family for the most part.

01:01:26.822 --> 01:01:29.723
Abortion was another form of birth control,

01:01:29.763 --> 01:01:34.946
but it was illegal and therefore very dangerous in the United States.

01:01:35.727 --> 01:01:37.548
I guess everywhere in that period,

01:01:37.628 --> 01:01:37.748
no?

01:01:38.368 --> 01:01:38.488
No.

01:01:39.029 --> 01:01:40.509
You could get an abortion more easily.

01:01:40.669 --> 01:01:41.210
For example,

01:01:41.211 --> 01:01:42.631
it was legal in the Soviet Union.

01:01:47.051 --> 01:01:47.231
Yes,

01:01:47.311 --> 01:01:49.993
so it was the illegal abortion which was so...

01:01:50.994 --> 01:01:51.554
Dangerous,

01:01:51.634 --> 01:01:53.135
the back alley abortions,

01:01:53.195 --> 01:01:53.415
yeah.

01:01:53.855 --> 01:01:54.656
I mean the idea,

01:01:54.856 --> 01:01:56.357
if you're going to plan a family,

01:01:56.617 --> 01:01:58.258
you don't want to plan it after the fact.

01:01:58.259 --> 01:02:02.441
You want to prepare ahead of time and during wartime when...

01:02:03.061 --> 01:02:06.622
husbands and wives warring together on a regular basis.

01:02:06.982 --> 01:02:08.162
The idea was,

01:02:08.622 --> 01:02:10.183
do you want to have a family now?

01:02:10.223 --> 01:02:11.843
Do you want to wait until the war is over?

01:02:12.483 --> 01:02:13.764
Those are considerations.

01:02:13.765 --> 01:02:17.884
So birth control becomes an even more important issue.

01:02:18.865 --> 01:02:18.985
Oh,

01:02:19.625 --> 01:02:19.765
yeah,

01:02:19.785 --> 01:02:20.685
I hadn't thought about that.

01:02:20.785 --> 01:02:21.005
Sure,

01:02:21.006 --> 01:02:24.386
if soldiers coming home on leave spend time with their wives,

01:02:24.526 --> 01:02:27.066
but do they want to leave their wives pregnant and alone,

01:02:27.707 --> 01:02:28.667
possibly widowed?

01:02:33.139 --> 01:02:37.181
The list of all the things they love about each other is far too long.

01:02:38.702 --> 01:02:39.842
One about the other,

01:02:41.063 --> 01:02:41.903
one with the other.

01:02:43.504 --> 01:02:46.505
If they had to make a list to register with a notary,

01:02:47.666 --> 01:02:48.807
it would take them ages.

01:02:50.626 --> 01:02:54.127
They'd have to note down every detail and record every caress.

01:02:56.148 --> 01:02:57.328
But that's not why they're here.

01:02:58.528 --> 01:02:59.309
They hold hands,

01:02:59.669 --> 01:03:00.589
slightly sullenly.

01:03:01.469 --> 01:03:02.610
Jean is sitting on the floor.

01:03:03.890 --> 01:03:05.771
What's his father registering with the notary?

01:03:07.071 --> 01:03:08.691
How would you explain it to a child?

01:03:09.512 --> 01:03:16.134
The child doesn't want to know that his father is off to war and that the business of the men in the dark suit is willed.

01:03:17.282 --> 01:03:18.182
But why make a will?

01:03:18.983 --> 01:03:20.364
Simon doesn't own anything,

01:03:21.185 --> 01:03:22.766
and he leaves everything to Lucienne.

01:03:23.906 --> 01:03:24.367
Everything.

01:03:25.488 --> 01:03:26.188
Meaning nothing.

01:04:38.786 --> 01:04:40.146
This fell next to him.

01:04:41.787 --> 01:04:41.987
Wow.

01:04:46.728 --> 01:04:48.548
Looks like a piece of shrapnel.

01:04:50.528 --> 01:04:51.869
And it fell right next to him,

01:04:51.950 --> 01:04:53.110
like 10 centimeters.

01:04:53.170 --> 01:04:55.191
So it's like he's not here,

01:04:55.291 --> 01:05:00.652
but half a foot away it would have been his plate.

01:05:01.553 --> 01:05:04.093
And that's one of the few memories he kept from

01:05:04.594 --> 01:05:05.054
Normandy.

01:05:08.670 --> 01:05:10.032
Then the other thing are very,

01:05:10.372 --> 01:05:10.512
oh,

01:05:10.613 --> 01:05:11.574
it's like,

01:05:11.874 --> 01:05:12.034
well,

01:05:12.515 --> 01:05:12.755
you know,

01:05:12.955 --> 01:05:13.296
this is,

01:05:14.057 --> 01:05:14.617
this would say,

01:05:14.678 --> 01:05:15.057
I guess,

01:05:15.138 --> 01:05:15.298
what,

01:05:15.759 --> 01:05:16.440
where he is,

01:05:16.620 --> 01:05:18.142
where he fought,

01:05:18.302 --> 01:05:18.422
or...

01:05:20.525 --> 01:05:21.766
There are a couple things on here.

01:05:22.606 --> 01:05:22.847
I mean,

01:05:22.868 --> 01:05:24.229
I can tell you've got blood type,

01:05:25.050 --> 01:05:25.271
but...

01:05:28.262 --> 01:05:28.802
Let's see...

01:05:29.403 --> 01:05:29.843
His name,

01:05:30.103 --> 01:05:30.483
obviously.

01:05:30.823 --> 01:05:31.044
Yeah,

01:05:31.264 --> 01:05:31.884
his name...

01:05:32.304 --> 01:05:32.644
Um,

01:05:32.825 --> 01:05:33.345
Simon J.

01:05:33.785 --> 01:05:34.205
Coppens?

01:05:34.686 --> 01:05:34.946
Right,

01:05:35.206 --> 01:05:35.526
okay.

01:05:36.086 --> 01:05:36.407
Um,

01:05:36.667 --> 01:05:37.287
it looks like,

01:05:37.847 --> 01:05:38.448
blood type A?

01:05:38.468 --> 01:05:40.369
Is that H?

01:05:42.750 --> 01:05:43.271
let me see.

01:05:46.853 --> 01:05:47.513
Is it H?

01:05:47.613 --> 01:05:47.853
Yes,

01:05:48.053 --> 01:05:48.233
yes.

01:05:48.393 --> 01:05:48.514
H?

01:05:48.515 --> 01:05:48.614
Yes,

01:05:48.834 --> 01:05:49.454
that's what I read,

01:05:49.534 --> 01:05:49.714
too.

01:05:52.436 --> 01:05:52.556
well,

01:05:52.596 --> 01:05:55.438
normally they have the religious designation on there as well.

01:05:56.318 --> 01:05:57.839
And was your father Jewish?

01:05:58.079 --> 01:05:58.479
Yeah.

01:05:58.480 --> 01:05:58.659
Okay.

01:05:59.159 --> 01:05:59.819
That's what it means.

01:06:00.120 --> 01:06:00.760
H is Hebrew.

01:06:01.580 --> 01:06:01.820
Okay.

01:06:01.821 --> 01:06:03.261
I didn't know that.

01:06:03.401 --> 01:06:03.521
Yeah.

01:06:04.181 --> 01:06:04.662
That's great.

01:06:04.902 --> 01:06:05.082
Well,

01:06:05.162 --> 01:06:05.502
thank you.

01:06:05.722 --> 01:06:06.962
Sure.

01:06:07.503 --> 01:06:07.763
You know,

01:06:07.903 --> 01:06:09.544
family memorabilia.

01:06:09.624 --> 01:06:12.505
Of course.

01:06:13.765 --> 01:06:14.346
Not that much.

01:06:14.726 --> 01:06:14.846
No?

01:06:14.847 --> 01:06:14.946
No.

01:06:16.309 --> 01:06:16.829
Surprisingly,

01:06:16.869 --> 01:06:17.150
I mean,

01:06:17.871 --> 01:06:18.311
every time,

01:06:18.490 --> 01:06:19.110
every once in a while,

01:06:19.170 --> 01:06:21.492
somebody will come to the office with the dog tag,

01:06:22.152 --> 01:06:23.532
and they want to find out information.

01:06:24.012 --> 01:06:24.632
Most of the time,

01:06:24.693 --> 01:06:25.632
we can't find anything.

01:06:26.612 --> 01:06:27.394
When I was in Belgium,

01:06:27.394 --> 01:06:30.114
there was one exception that I found.

01:06:31.735 --> 01:06:32.175
A lady,

01:06:32.195 --> 01:06:34.016
a Belgian lady brought me in a box,

01:06:34.556 --> 01:06:35.615
a wooden box one day,

01:06:36.557 --> 01:06:39.798
and it had photographs and letters.

01:06:40.978 --> 01:06:41.739
They were all in English,

01:06:42.420 --> 01:06:44.581
and it had an address on the outside of the box.

01:06:45.163 --> 01:06:50.488
And she told me that there was an American soldier living in her family's house during the war,

01:06:51.289 --> 01:06:52.390
and when the soldier left,

01:06:52.391 --> 01:06:53.751
he left the box behind.

01:06:53.771 --> 01:06:57.095
It was love letters from him to his wife and photographs.

01:06:57.816 --> 01:06:58.096
And so...

01:07:00.839 --> 01:07:01.159
I mean,

01:07:01.779 --> 01:07:04.120
most of the time when people bring stuff like that to us,

01:07:04.180 --> 01:07:05.801
we never find out who it belongs to.

01:07:06.781 --> 01:07:06.981
But

01:07:08.342 --> 01:07:09.502
I saw it had Niagara Falls,

01:07:09.562 --> 01:07:11.183
New York on the top of the box.

01:07:12.203 --> 01:07:15.365
So I called the clerk of court's office in Niagara Falls.

01:07:15.885 --> 01:07:16.645
I talked to the lady.

01:07:16.665 --> 01:07:17.085
I said,

01:07:17.506 --> 01:07:17.766
you know,

01:07:17.767 --> 01:07:19.446
I work at Arden American Cemetery.

01:07:19.546 --> 01:07:20.487
I have a name here.

01:07:21.127 --> 01:07:21.447
She said,

01:07:21.467 --> 01:07:22.928
I'm going to do something I'm not supposed to do.

01:07:23.928 --> 01:07:27.390
But she went through her files and she found out some information for me.

01:07:28.175 --> 01:07:29.836
And I had to make about 10 phone calls,

01:07:29.876 --> 01:07:30.557
but I finally,

01:07:31.357 --> 01:07:32.918
I talked to the guy's son,

01:07:33.158 --> 01:07:34.199
who was living in Florida,

01:07:35.340 --> 01:07:37.942
and his father was still alive.

01:07:38.522 --> 01:07:39.103
The next day,

01:07:39.423 --> 01:07:40.624
the box was gone.

01:07:40.625 --> 01:07:41.264
It was in Florida.

01:07:42.465 --> 01:07:46.568
And it arrived on the anniversary of the husband and wife,

01:07:46.708 --> 01:07:50.691
and so they got all the pictures and letters and everything back that they had lost for

01:07:51.051 --> 01:07:51.931
65 years.

01:07:52.732 --> 01:07:54.934
And when those kind of moments,

01:07:55.494 --> 01:07:56.615
that still makes me want to cry,

01:07:56.635 --> 01:07:56.895
you know?

01:07:57.499 --> 01:07:58.240
When I,

01:07:58.620 --> 01:08:01.802
to be able to help this veteran with something like that,

01:08:01.822 --> 01:08:03.323
it was an amazing moment.

01:08:03.583 --> 01:08:05.925
And I just found out he died a couple weeks ago.

01:08:06.666 --> 01:08:09.708
His son sent me an email saying thank you for what you did for my father.

01:08:10.448 --> 01:08:13.551
Because those kind of things normally don't have happy endings.

01:08:14.051 --> 01:08:17.434
When people bring me a dog tag or something like a helmet or something,

01:08:17.954 --> 01:08:19.095
most of the time you can't find,

01:08:19.535 --> 01:08:19.775
you know,

01:08:19.875 --> 01:08:21.176
you can't find any information.

01:08:21.256 --> 01:08:23.778
But that was just,

01:08:23.898 --> 01:08:24.779
I just got lucky.

01:08:57.105 --> 01:08:57.265
June

01:08:57.266 --> 01:08:57.305
5th,

01:08:57.306 --> 01:09:00.607
1994.

01:09:00.608 --> 01:09:01.087
For Richard.

01:09:02.087 --> 01:09:08.490
Lucienne wanted our children to be able to say that their father had participated in the war against fascism.

01:09:10.131 --> 01:09:10.411
Love,

01:09:11.315 --> 01:09:11.475
What

01:09:14.357 --> 01:09:16.939
Simon cannot tell,

01:09:16.940 --> 01:09:19.700
but will be when they will meet again the object of endless stories.

01:09:19.820 --> 01:09:22.962
It is the way he fought in France,

01:09:23.783 --> 01:09:24.423
like a soldier,

01:09:24.424 --> 01:09:25.924
but without weapons.

01:09:26.905 --> 01:09:27.945
He fought with words,

01:09:27.946 --> 01:09:29.726
we have already said,

01:09:29.746 --> 01:09:30.707
but also with notes,

01:09:31.549 --> 01:09:33.149
On June 26,

01:09:33.150 --> 01:09:34.109
1944,

01:09:34.750 --> 01:09:36.230
Simon descended from a ship.

01:09:37.211 --> 01:09:40.493
He got on a small truck equipped with a speaker,

01:09:41.194 --> 01:09:45.577
a microphone and a 78-round battery.

01:09:45.578 --> 01:09:47.258
In the cities and villages liberated,

01:09:48.198 --> 01:09:50.840
where the Germans had confiscated the radio stations,

01:09:51.520 --> 01:09:53.782
Simon stopped.

01:09:53.842 --> 01:09:57.244
A jazz music came out from the speaker.

01:09:57.264 --> 01:09:59.065
People gathered.

01:09:59.066 --> 01:10:00.866
A voice gave information.

01:10:01.967 --> 01:10:04.468
Information that at first was not good,

01:10:04.508 --> 01:10:05.909
and then good,

01:10:05.949 --> 01:10:07.529
and then very good.

01:10:07.569 --> 01:10:09.350
News from the front,

01:10:10.010 --> 01:10:11.691
and then the Marseillaise,

01:10:12.612 --> 01:10:13.672
and then the applause.

01:10:13.673 --> 01:10:16.793
Is this called propaganda?

01:10:17.834 --> 01:10:18.494
Propaganda,

01:10:18.794 --> 01:10:19.474
to propagate,

01:10:19.475 --> 01:10:19.955
to spread,

01:10:20.055 --> 01:10:20.415
to spread.

01:10:20.416 --> 01:10:24.477
The light spreads in a straight line,

01:10:24.478 --> 01:10:26.457
the sound in every direction.

01:10:26.497 --> 01:10:26.758
And

01:10:27.658 --> 01:10:29.519
Simon shines the region in every direction.

01:10:32.519 --> 01:10:34.560
Next to it is the truck.

01:10:40.883 --> 01:10:46.626
The truck that gives information about the liberation of Normandy.

01:10:46.806 --> 01:10:49.008
In the cities and villages.

01:10:49.188 --> 01:10:54.310
It's a nice document.

01:10:57.567 --> 01:10:59.149
Do you recognize the truck?

01:10:59.150 --> 01:10:59.269
Yes,

01:10:59.289 --> 01:11:02.231
we recognize the truck on its right.

01:11:02.411 --> 01:11:03.852
It's a Dodge.

01:11:04.393 --> 01:11:05.854
We can see the speaker on the side.

01:11:05.934 --> 01:11:06.334
On its left,

01:11:06.354 --> 01:11:09.356
there are trucks.

01:11:09.376 --> 01:11:09.717
It's a

01:11:10.437 --> 01:11:12.179
GMC on its left.

01:11:13.883 --> 01:11:17.245
That was his journey.

01:11:17.265 --> 01:11:19.066
He was here for two months.

01:11:19.106 --> 01:11:21.208
From June 22 to August 20,

01:11:21.209 --> 01:11:22.068
approximately.

01:11:22.268 --> 01:11:24.750
Based in Colombian.

01:11:24.790 --> 01:11:29.753
He marked the names of the cities where he went.

01:11:29.754 --> 01:11:30.353
He went there every day,

01:11:30.354 --> 01:11:32.255
giving news regularly.

01:11:32.315 --> 01:11:32.775
Saint-Laurent,

01:11:32.815 --> 01:11:33.315
Colombian,

01:11:33.375 --> 01:11:33.876
Rézigny,

01:11:33.976 --> 01:11:34.476
Carentan,

01:11:34.556 --> 01:11:35.297
Saint-Néry-Eglise,

01:11:35.337 --> 01:11:35.717
Montbourg,

01:11:35.797 --> 01:11:36.157
Valogne,

01:11:36.277 --> 01:11:36.697
Cherbourg,

01:11:37.718 --> 01:11:38.198
Bregbec.

01:11:39.675 --> 01:11:40.235
Carteret,

01:11:40.275 --> 01:11:40.896
Barneville,

01:11:41.396 --> 01:11:42.757
Saint Sauveur-le-Viponte,

01:11:43.877 --> 01:11:44.597
La Haye-du-Puy,

01:11:45.618 --> 01:11:45.958
Lecce,

01:11:46.338 --> 01:11:46.758
Perrier,

01:11:48.139 --> 01:11:48.839
Coutances,

01:11:49.820 --> 01:11:51.761
direction Grandville and Coutainville.

01:11:51.762 --> 01:11:55.483
He had traveled the region well.

01:12:00.824 --> 01:12:02.345
Yes,

01:12:02.365 --> 01:12:03.666
so all this comes from...

01:12:03.667 --> 01:12:04.907
It comes from that.

01:12:04.908 --> 01:12:06.288
It comes from his road map.

01:12:06.348 --> 01:12:07.589
He started...

01:12:07.590 --> 01:12:08.349
It's not very regular.

01:12:10.571 --> 01:12:13.253
He gives quite a bit of precision on the places,

01:12:13.333 --> 01:12:15.094
for example Notre-Dame de...

01:12:16.436 --> 01:12:16.876
Seville,

01:12:16.877 --> 01:12:17.456
I think.

01:12:17.476 --> 01:12:17.797
He says,

01:12:17.798 --> 01:12:18.897
we were parked near the post,

01:12:18.898 --> 01:12:20.338
in front of the post.

01:12:20.358 --> 01:12:21.379
There were about

01:12:23.461 --> 01:12:24.942
100 people.

01:12:31.671 --> 01:12:32.131
The B&amp;B also,

01:12:32.171 --> 01:12:37.293
100-150 people,

01:12:37.294 --> 01:12:42.154
so it gives about the population density.

01:12:42.155 --> 01:12:42.714
It says here,

01:12:43.394 --> 01:12:44.335
very good reception,

01:12:45.355 --> 01:12:46.995
enjoy playing the Marseillaise.

01:13:12.256 --> 01:13:13.417
Chateau de Colombieres,

01:13:14.057 --> 01:13:16.299
July 17th 1944.

01:13:17.900 --> 01:13:18.441
My great,

01:13:18.861 --> 01:13:21.323
beloved light and my dear sun.

01:13:23.505 --> 01:13:25.226
Exactly 13 years ago today,

01:13:26.167 --> 01:13:30.010
I boarded the boat in New York to travel to France for the first time.

01:13:31.299 --> 01:13:36.841
I had no way of knowing that that voyage would change my whole life and all my plans.

01:13:37.782 --> 01:13:43.724
That I'd never become a lawyer and that I'd return to France in 1938 to find my Lucienne.

01:13:44.985 --> 01:13:48.026
Neither did I know that I would fall in love with France.

01:13:55.709 --> 01:13:57.029
My love is so wonderful,

01:13:57.410 --> 01:13:57.950
my life.

01:13:59.110 --> 01:14:00.111
I miss you so much.

01:14:01.135 --> 01:14:01.555
Kissing,

01:14:02.956 --> 01:14:04.457
our great and wonderful passion.

01:14:06.619 --> 01:14:07.739
Soon I shall go to bed,

01:14:08.740 --> 01:14:11.161
and in my thoughts I shall snuggle into your arms,

01:14:12.102 --> 01:14:14.304
to feel the exquisite pleasure of your warm,

01:14:14.684 --> 01:14:15.344
loving body.

01:14:16.445 --> 01:14:16.765
Quick,

01:14:17.706 --> 01:14:18.366
hold me tight,

01:14:18.406 --> 01:14:19.047
my Lucienne.

01:14:19.847 --> 01:14:20.187
Quick,

01:14:21.008 --> 01:14:21.888
all your caresses,

01:14:21.948 --> 01:14:22.469
your lips,

01:14:22.529 --> 01:14:23.089
your tongue,

01:14:23.149 --> 01:14:24.670
above all your beloved hand.

01:14:26.695 --> 01:14:28.356
Your beloved hand.

01:14:28.376 --> 01:14:30.297
And quick I want to be deeply yours.

01:14:31.377 --> 01:14:32.197
For our ecstasy.

01:14:33.198 --> 01:14:34.218
To hold your eyes.

01:14:35.059 --> 01:14:35.699
And your joy.

01:14:41.021 --> 01:14:41.961
I love you so much,

01:14:42.021 --> 01:14:42.602
my Lucien.

01:14:43.242 --> 01:14:44.002
And I am with you.

01:14:47.144 --> 01:14:47.604
Your Sim.

01:15:02.775 --> 01:15:07.619
So this is a letter my father wrote to my mother to explain his postal address.

01:15:08.399 --> 01:15:11.202
You will read at the top and we will see if it is here.

01:15:11.342 --> 01:15:11.482
No

01:15:12.463 --> 01:15:15.285
Wait, second mobile radio broadcasting is not true.

01:15:16.185 --> 01:15:17.246
Here is the meaning

01:15:18.247 --> 01:15:20.168
Headquarters First US Army,

01:15:20.848 --> 01:15:23.289
Publicity and Psychological Warfare,

01:15:23.949 --> 01:15:26.390
Second Mobile Radio Broadcasting Company.

01:15:27.130 --> 01:15:29.531
Second Mobile Radio Broadcasting Company.

01:15:30.051 --> 01:15:31.871
Ça ne pouvait pas être ailleurs que ici.

01:15:33.512 --> 01:15:34.732
J'ai souligné les lettres.

01:15:35.433 --> 01:15:36.693
Serre-toi des initiales.

01:15:38.454 --> 01:15:42.315
Tout ceci te donnera peut-être une meilleure idée de mes fonctions.

01:15:43.423 --> 01:15:44.084
My beloved,

01:15:44.104 --> 01:15:44.524
I must leave.

01:15:44.605 --> 01:15:45.446
Maybe on the road,

01:15:45.447 --> 01:15:46.807
I will have a moment to add something,

01:15:46.808 --> 01:15:47.328
maybe not.

01:15:47.368 --> 01:15:48.129
Know this,

01:15:48.649 --> 01:15:50.011
I take care of myself,

01:15:50.031 --> 01:15:51.172
my health is excellent,

01:15:51.212 --> 01:15:54.516
my work is a continuous joy.

01:15:54.837 --> 01:15:57.800
Despite inevitable troubles that do not come from your people.

01:15:58.481 --> 01:16:01.404
The welcome of your people is very affectionate.

01:16:01.405 --> 01:16:02.786
Their attitude is splendid.

01:16:03.747 --> 01:16:04.067
Really,

01:16:04.068 --> 01:16:07.329
the French are happy to see us.

01:16:07.330 --> 01:16:11.671
I heard that the national hymns of all the allies are being applauded,

01:16:11.672 --> 01:16:12.811
and apparently,

01:16:12.851 --> 01:16:13.152
especially,

01:16:13.172 --> 01:16:15.593
the Russian ally.

01:16:15.594 --> 01:16:15.693
Here,

01:16:15.694 --> 01:16:16.893
I have two photos of my father,

01:16:16.913 --> 01:16:19.415
one with a friend and one with another.

01:16:24.507 --> 01:16:24.887
So this one.

01:16:24.907 --> 01:16:33.473
I would like to mention on this photo the sign that your father wears,

01:16:35.015 --> 01:16:36.676
which attracts my attention.

01:16:37.897 --> 01:16:39.798
It's the famous sign of the OSS,

01:16:39.818 --> 01:16:42.220
which is now called the CIA.

01:16:43.761 --> 01:16:45.702
So he was well into the psychological war.

01:16:55.568 --> 01:16:56.121
Thank you.

01:18:20.752 --> 01:18:22.173
Simon is already in Paris,

01:18:22.613 --> 01:18:24.054
but Lucienne is still in New York.

01:18:28.537 --> 01:18:29.918
She receives a letter every day,

01:18:30.919 --> 01:18:31.919
two on a good day.

01:18:33.140 --> 01:18:34.081
She sends photos,

01:18:34.801 --> 01:18:39.064
which Simon slides into glassine paper pouches inside a small notebook.

01:19:13.546 --> 01:19:13.746
Some

01:19:13.786 --> 01:19:14.166
nights,

01:19:14.746 --> 01:19:16.047
rather than filling the void,

01:19:16.947 --> 01:19:19.488
the tenderest words simply enlarge it.

01:19:21.652 --> 01:19:23.453
The letters overflow with affection.

01:19:24.593 --> 01:19:27.114
Lucienne drowns in her little bed on Long Island.

01:19:28.594 --> 01:19:30.094
So she clings to the pictures.

01:19:40.297 --> 01:19:43.818
What became of all the people who were on the haystack with her that day?

01:19:45.839 --> 01:19:48.560
She thinks of all those who are gone forever.

01:19:50.224 --> 01:19:51.264
You'll never see them again.

01:19:55.366 --> 01:19:56.046
In France,

01:19:56.326 --> 01:19:59.867
Simon seeks to secure Lucien's return to Noéville.

01:20:01.048 --> 01:20:02.448
His days are very busy though.

01:20:03.309 --> 01:20:05.209
He gives talks about life in America,

01:20:05.910 --> 01:20:07.950
celebrates the anniversary of the Commune,

01:20:08.491 --> 01:20:10.971
and listens to the Red Army Choir in Cologne.

01:20:16.140 --> 01:20:23.127
He collects evidence of their future life as though every step and every movement forged a link between them.

01:20:27.051 --> 01:20:28.472
They have to wait another month,

01:20:29.513 --> 01:20:30.014
two months,

01:20:31.155 --> 01:20:35.459
fourteen months before Lucien is granted permission to return to France.

01:20:37.124 --> 01:20:40.406
Jean has turned three by the time she steps off the boat in Le Havre.

01:20:41.287 --> 01:20:41.767
Naturally,

01:20:42.288 --> 01:20:43.088
everyone weeps,

01:20:43.549 --> 01:20:45.530
and we could simply stop the camera there.

01:20:46.551 --> 01:20:47.651
On the scared little boy,

01:20:47.732 --> 01:20:49.633
they reassure with fairytale words,

01:20:50.253 --> 01:20:51.894
they'll never be separated again.

01:20:53.035 --> 01:20:53.335
Never.

01:20:54.857 --> 01:20:55.637
And for once,

01:20:56.338 --> 01:20:57.618
this turns out to be true.

01:20:58.439 --> 01:20:59.980
They are never separated again.

01:21:00.981 --> 01:21:03.042
They live happily and have many children.

01:21:03.883 --> 01:21:04.083
Well,

01:21:04.884 --> 01:21:05.244
one more.

01:21:08.060 --> 01:21:09.641
The story fades into memories.

01:21:10.781 --> 01:21:13.242
No need to invent words to bridge the distance.

01:21:13.982 --> 01:21:14.963
No need for tales.

01:21:15.543 --> 01:21:16.563
It's enough to say...

01:21:17.383 --> 01:21:18.044
I remember.

01:21:22.085 --> 01:21:24.646
I remember that Burt Lancaster was an acrobat.

01:21:26.547 --> 01:21:29.068
I remember Sem Copin's jazz programs.

01:21:30.844 --> 01:21:35.628
I remember that my mother never wanted me to use Simon's letter in a radio program.

01:21:36.489 --> 01:21:38.731
She regarded them as too private.

01:21:40.353 --> 01:21:43.976
I remember November 1956.

01:21:43.977 --> 01:21:44.436
I was nine.

01:21:44.437 --> 01:21:45.517
I heard my mother shout,

01:21:45.858 --> 01:21:47.699
the fascists are attacking the party.

01:21:48.961 --> 01:21:52.063
I remember that my father never wanted to become French.

01:21:53.464 --> 01:21:55.905
I remember that at the end of the Algerian War,

01:21:56.225 --> 01:22:02.747
my father was afraid of being expelled from France because my brother was a sympathizer of the FLN support networks.

01:22:04.147 --> 01:22:09.969
I remember two dinners at home with an attaché from the Soviet embassy in the middle of the Cold War.

01:22:10.909 --> 01:22:12.570
And I've always harbored doubts.

01:22:13.110 --> 01:22:14.670
Was my father a double agent?

01:22:15.151 --> 01:22:18.832
Was the FBI covering for him when they told me his file was empty?

01:22:19.852 --> 01:22:26.014
I remember going three times to the Palais des Sports at the Port de Versailles to see the Red Army Choir.

01:22:26.654 --> 01:22:28.914
My mother bought me a recording of Kalinka.

01:22:29.955 --> 01:22:33.356
I remember that my father got every newly released record,

01:22:33.936 --> 01:22:38.517
and I was allowed to sell bad French music to my high school friends on the cheap.

01:22:39.597 --> 01:22:47.539
I remember the jam jar in which every day my mother would put the money for the two packets of Pall Mall my parents no longer smoked.

01:22:48.220 --> 01:22:49.660
those savings paid for the first

01:22:49.660 --> 01:22:51.461
furniture in our house in Lanzac.

01:22:52.861 --> 01:23:00.023
I remember that my mother watched over my father's diet three times a day for 27 years to control his diabetes.

01:23:01.083 --> 01:23:04.584
He was allowed one piece of chocolate a week and an apple a day.

01:23:06.405 --> 01:23:13.627
I remember their enthusiasm when they returned from their first trip beyond the Iron Curtain to Budapest in 1986.

01:23:14.667 --> 01:23:16.428
Every market had apples.

01:23:19.360 --> 01:23:22.703
I remember one spring afternoon in 1972,

01:23:23.704 --> 01:23:28.488
my father frantically buttoning up his pajamas and shutting their bedroom doors.

01:23:30.710 --> 01:23:33.793
I don't remember if Simon and Lucienne wore wedding rings.

01:23:34.894 --> 01:23:37.196
I have to call my brother and ask him.

01:23:39.057 --> 01:23:46.383
I remember that the Suyak Jazz Festival decided to rename itself the Simcoe Pants Jazz Festival in 1985.

01:23:49.292 --> 01:23:51.974
Here we say they were fighting each other,

01:23:53.074 --> 01:23:54.395
they admired each other a little,

01:23:54.475 --> 01:23:55.636
they were fighting each other,

01:23:55.637 --> 01:23:56.297
that was a bit it.

01:23:56.337 --> 01:24:00.679
They were surprised in attitudes that were love attitudes.

01:24:00.779 --> 01:24:03.261
Well,

01:24:03.262 --> 01:24:04.722
they had discretion that is good,

01:24:04.742 --> 01:24:13.488
but it had struck us at the time.

01:24:14.188 --> 01:24:15.168
Maybe not mentioned,

01:24:15.288 --> 01:24:16.989
but really,

01:24:16.990 --> 01:24:17.790
we felt them close,

01:24:17.791 --> 01:24:19.270
they held each other by the hand.

01:24:19.550 --> 01:24:22.011
When they were going to walk,

01:24:22.091 --> 01:24:22.912
it was touching.

01:24:22.913 --> 01:24:25.653
It could be an example.

01:24:25.753 --> 01:24:25.873
Yes,

01:24:25.874 --> 01:24:28.554
because we are not the same.

01:24:37.804 --> 01:24:44.147
For many years there was corn in the field between the house and the river where my father played baseball in 1945.

01:24:45.147 --> 01:24:46.628
Then they planted walnut trees.

01:24:48.909 --> 01:24:50.389
But it's rained a lot this summer.

01:24:51.210 --> 01:24:54.911
The neighbors complain the harvest will be later than usual.

